            "description": "## Request body parameters\n\n| Parameter | Type | Description | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `subtask_ids` | array | Specify the Subtask ID. The integer value in the response of Fetch all subtasks for a DSR is the value for Subtask ID. For example, the `subtask ID` is 62733 in this response sample. | `[62733, 62734, 62735]` |\n| `rule` | string | Specify the rule as `inclusion` to apply updates (such as adding new subtask owners or changing subtask status) only to the listed subtasks, or `exclusion` to apply them to all except the listed ones. | `inclusion` |\n| `updates.field` | string | The field can be set to one of the following: `owner`: Use this to add, reassign, or remove a subtask owner from the subtasks listed in the `subtask_ids` array. `sub_processor`: Use this to add sub-processor details to subtasks linked to DSR requests of type `Disclosure of sub-processors`. `process`: Use this to attach process records to subtasks for DSR requests of type `access` or `port`. `status`: Use this to update the subtask status, such as marking it as approved, declined, errored, etc. | `owner` |\n| `updates.operation` | string | Defines the specific action to perform on the selected field. The allowed value for `operation` depends on the `field` chosen. Supported values by field: If `field` is `owner`: \"assign\", \"add\", or \"remove\". If `field` is `sub_processor`: \"add\". If `field` is `process`: \"process_record\". If `field` is `status`: \"update\". | `add` |\n| `updates.type` | string | Specify the type. Accepted values based on use case: `\"array\"`: Use when providing a list of items for example, multiple user IDs for assigning, adding, or removing subtask owners. `\"string\"`: Use when providing a single text-based value, such as a process record ID. `\"number\"`: Use when providing a numeric code — for example, when updating the status of a subtask. `\"object\"`: Use when declining a subtask. | `array` |\n| `updates.value` | array | Specify the value parameter based on the selected field and operation: `owner` + `assign`/`add`/`remove`: Provide an array of subtask owners IDs. Example: `[68, 298, 463, 34]`. `sub_processor` + `add`: Provide an array of objects with keys: recipient, recipient_type, purpose, legal_basis. Example: `[{\"recipient\":\"abc\",\"recipient_type\":\"xyz\",\"purpose\":\"test\",\"legal_basis\":\"lawful\"}]`. `process` + `process_record`: Provide a process record ID(fetch_all_process_records) as a string. The `process record ID` is 12234 in this response sample. `status` + `update`: Provide a numeric status code. Set the value for `value` to: `1`: to set the subtask in `Review` state, `3`: to set the subtask in `approve` state, `5`: to delete a subtask if no data is found, `7`: to set the subtask status to `error`. **Note:** For `decline`: Specify the `value` in the format given in the example below: `{\"status\":4,\"value\":{\"reason_subject\":\"reason for declining\",\"reason\":\"  We are unable to proceed with this subtask due to legal obligations  \",\"notes\":\"\"}}` | `[274,123,456]` |\n| `request_type` | integer | Specify the request_type. Set the following values for system supported request types: `1` for subtask of type Access, `2` for subtask of type Port, `3` for subtask of type Erasure, `4` for subtask of type Rectification, `5` for subtask of type Restrict Processing, `6` for subtask of type Objection, `7` for subtask of type Restrict Automated Individual Decision-making, `8` for subtask of type Do Not Sell, `10` for subtask of type Confirmation, `11` for subtask of type Disclosure of Subprocessors. For custom request types, use Fetch All Request Types API to obtain the correct request id. The `type_id` in the response body of Fetch All Request Types is the value for `request_type`. **Note:** The API returns both system and custom types, but system types always retain the fixed values listed above. | `2` |"
