              "body": "{\n    \"status\": 0,\n    \"message\": \"\",\n    \"data\": [\n        {\n            \"application\": {\n                \"covered_entities\": {\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.140(d)\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) Any sole proprietorship, partnership, limited liability company, corporation, association, or other legal entity that is organized or operated for the profit or financial benefit of its shareholders or other owners that conducts business in the state of California and which collects protected consumers' personal information and jointly or solely determines the means of it's processing and meets any one of these conditions:\\n\\n(a) Has annual gross revenues in excess of twenty-six million, six hundred and twenty-five thousand dollars ($26,625,000) in the preceding calendar year (As of January 1st).\\n\\n(b) Annually buys or, sells, or shares the personal information of 100,000 or more consumers, households.\\n\\n(c) Derives 50 percent or more of its annual revenues from selling or sharing consumers’ personal information.\\n\\n(2) Any entity that controls or is controlled by a business, and that shares common branding with the business and with whom the business shares consumers' personal information.\\n\\n(a) “Control” or “controlled” means ownership of, or the power to vote, more than 50 percent of the outstanding shares of any class of voting security of a business; control in any manner over the election of a majority of the directors, or of individuals exercising similar functions; or the power to exercise a controlling influence over the management of a company. \\n\\n(b) “Common branding” means a shared name, servicemark, or trademark that the average consumer would understand that two or more entities are commonly owned.\\n\\n(3)  A joint venture or partnership composed of businesses in which each business has at least a 40 percent interest and each business within this joint venture will be considered as a separate single business.\\n\\n(4) A person that does business in California, that does not fall under the given thresholds and that voluntarily certifies to the California Privacy Protection Agency that it is in compliance with, and agrees to be bound by CPRA.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\"\n                },\n                \"date_of_application\": {\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA\",\n                    \"date\": \"1 January 2023\",\n                    \"details\": \"This Act will become operative 1 January 2023, with the exception of the right of access, which shall only apply to personal information collected by a business on or after 1 January 2022.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://www.caprivacy.org/cpra-timeline/\"\n                },\n                \"date_of_enforcement\": {\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA\",\n                    \"date\": \"1 July 2023\",\n                    \"details\": \"The date of enforcement is 1 July 2023.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://www.caprivacy.org/cpra-timeline/\"\n                },\n                \"type_of_processing\": {\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.140(e) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(g) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(7)\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) Collecting, receiving, retaining, processing, sharing and selling of protected consumers' personal information for commercial or business purposes when any part of the commercial conduct, in the pursuance of which the personal information was collected, took place in California.\\n\\n(2) Commercial purposes are to advance a person’s commercial or economic interests, such as by inducing another person to buy, rent, lease, join, subscribe to, provide, or exchange products, goods, property, information, or services, or enabling or effecting, directly or indirectly, a commercial transaction. \\n\\n(3) Business purposes means the use of personal information for the business’ or a service provider’s operational purposes, or other notified purposes, or for the service provider or contractor's operational purposes, as defined by regulations adopted pursuant to CPRA provided that the use of personal information shall be reasonably necessary and proportionate to achieve the purpose for which the personal information was collected or processed or for another purpose that is compatible with the context in which the personal information was collected. Business purposes are:\\n\\n(a) Auditing related to counting ad impressions to unique visitors, verifying positioning and quality of ad impressions, and auditing compliance with this specification and other standards.\\n\\n(b) Helping to ensure security and integrity to the extent the use of the consumer's personal information is reasonably necessary and proportionate for these purposes.\\n\\n(c) Debugging to identify and repair errors that impair existing intended functionality.\\n\\n(d) Short-term, transient use,  including but not limited to non-personalized advertising shown as part of a consumer's current interaction with the business, provided that the personal information is not disclosed to another third party and is not used to build a profile about the consumer or otherwise alter the consumer’s experience outside the current interaction with the business.\\n\\n(e) Performing services on behalf of the business, including maintaining or servicing accounts, providing customer service, processing or fulfilling orders and transactions, verifying customer information, processing payments, providing financing, providing analytic services, providing storage or providing similar services on behalf of the business.\\n\\n(f)  Providing advertising and marketing services, except for cross-context behavioral advertising, to the consumer, provided that for the purpose of advertising and marketing, a service provider or contractor shall not combine the personal information of opted-out consumers which the service provider or contractor receives from or on behalf of the business with personal information which the service provider or contractor receives from or on behalf · of another person or persons, or collects from its own interaction with consumers.\\n\\n(g) Undertaking internal research for technological development and demonstration.\\n\\n(h) Undertaking activities to verify or maintain the quality or safety of a service or device that is owned, manufactured, manufactured for, or controlled by the business, and to improve, upgrade, or enhance the service or device that is owned, manufactured, manufactured for, or controlled by the business.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\"\n                }\n            },\n            \"category\": \"private\",\n            \"country\": \"United States of America\",\n            \"cure_provisions\": {\n                \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.199.45.\",\n                \"cure_provision_defenses\": \"\",\n                \"cure_provision_defenses_citation\": \"\",\n                \"details\": \"(1) Upon receiving the sworn complaint of any person or on its own initiative, the CPPA may decide to provide a business with a time period to cure the alleged violation. \\n\\n(2) In making a decision to provide more time to cure, the agency may consider the following:\\n\\n(a) Lack of intent to violate this law.\\n\\n(b) Voluntary efforts undertaken by the business, service provider, contractor, or person to cure the alleged violation prior to being notified by the CPPA of the complaint.\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\"\n            },\n            \"data_subjects_right\": [\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToInformation\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"\",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.100(b) , US CPRA Section 1798.110(c) , US CPRA Section 1798.115(c)  ,  US CPRA Section 1798.115(d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7010\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) The covered entity is required to provide a notice at or before collection of personal information and sensitive personal information.  \\n\\n(2) The covered entity may not collect any personal information or use it or disclose it for any other purpose beyond what it discloses in the notice to the consumer or sell it if it has not disclosed that in the notice.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"The covered entity is required to disclose details of its information privacy practices in its California specific privacy policy.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A third party shall not sell or share personal information about a consumer that has been sold to, or shared with, the third party by a business unless the consumer has received explicit notice.\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"no\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"\",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7012 (h)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"A business that neither collects nor controls the collection of personal information directly from the consumer does not need to provide a Notice at Collection to the consumer if it neither sells nor shares the consumer’s personal information. \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Exemption from notice at point of collection\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"A data broker can avail this exemption only if it has included in its registration submission a link to its online privacy policy that includes instructions on how a consumer can submit a request to opt-out of sale/sharing. \",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) The data broker must be registered with the Attorney General pursuant to Civil Code section 1798.99.80 et seq.,\\n\\n(2) The data broker must be collecting personal information from a source other than directly from the consumer.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7012 (h)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"A data broker does not need to provide a Notice at Collection to the\\nconsumer.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Data Broker Exemption\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.100(b) , US CPRA Section 1798.110(c) , US CPRA Section 1798.115(c)  ,  US CPRA Section 1798.115(d)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"(1) Consumers have a right to be informed about the privacy practices of the covered entity and what personal information is about to be collected on or before the point of collection\\n\\n(2) Consumers have a right to be informed about what personal information, sensitive personal information is collected by a business, from which sources, how long the information is retained and the onward disclosures of their personal information collected about them by the covered entity to third parties.\\n\\n(3) Consumers have right to be informed when a third party is selling personal information about them which was gained from a covered entity. \",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to receive information on privacy practices and onward disclosures\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": []\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToAccess\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"(1) No later than 10 business days after receiving a request a business shall confirm receipt of the request and provide information about how the business will process the request. The information provided shall describe in general the business’s verification process and\\nwhen the consumer should expect a response, except in instances where the business has already granted or denied the request. \\n\\n(2)  In response to an access request the business must respond with:\\n\\n(a) Specific pieces of personal information that a business has collected about the consumer;\\n\\n(b) Categories of personal information it has collected about the consumer;\\n\\n(c) Categories of sources from which the personal information is collected;\\n\\n(d) Categories of personal information that the business sold or disclosed for a business purpose about the consumer;\\n\\n(e) Categories of third parties to whom the personal information was sold or disclosed for a business purpose; and \\n\\n(f) The business or commercial purpose for collecting or selling personal information. \",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.110 , US CPRA Section 1798.115 , US CPRA Regulations § 7002 (z) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (k) , \",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(3) , US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(4) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (j)\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A business that receives a verifiable consumer request shall disclose any personal information it has collected about a consumer, directly or indirectly, including through or by a service provider or contractor, to the consumer.\\n\\n(2) A service provider or contractor shall not be required to comply with a verifiable consumer request received directly from a consumer or a consumer's authorized agent to the extent that the service provider or contractor has collected personal information about the consumer in its role as a service provider or contractor. A service provider or contractor shall provide assistance to a business with which it has a contractual relationship with respect to the business's response to a verifiable consumer request, Including but not limited to by providing to the business the consumer's personal information in the service provider or contractor's possession, which the service provider or contractor obtained as a result of providing services to the business\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"If businesses collect personal information of consumers for business or commercial purposes, they are required to identify:\\n\\n(1) the consumer, associate the information provided by the consumer in the verifiable consumer request to any personal information previously collected by the business about the consumer.\\n\\n(2) the personal information collected about the consumer, by category or categories, during the applicable period of time by reference to the enumerated category or categories of personal information and sensitive personal information described within the law that most closely describe the personal information collected;\\n\\n(2) the categories of sources from which the consumer's personal information was collected; \\n\\n(3) the business or commercial purpose for collecting, or selling or sharing the consumer's personal information; and \\n\\n(4) the categories of third parties to whom the business discloses the consumer's personal information.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"If the consumer requests for access to specific pieces of information:\\n\\n(1) Businesses are required to provide the specific pieces of personal information obtained from the consumer in a format that is easily understandable to the average consumer, and to the extent technically feasible, in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format, which also may be transmitted to another entity at the consumer's request without hindrance. \\n\\n(2) \\\"Specific pieces of information\\\" do not include data generated to help ensure security and integrity or as prescribed by regulation. Personal information is not considered to have been disclosed by a business when a consumer instructs a business to transfer the consumer's personal information from one business to another in the context of switching services.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"If they sell or share personal information of consumers. businesses are required to:\\n\\n(1) Identify the consumer and associate the information provided by the consumer in the verifiable consumer request to any personal information previously collected by the business about the consumer.\\n\\n(2) Identify by category or categories the personal information of the consumer that the business sold or shared during the applicable period of time by reference to the enumerated categories of personal information and sensitive personal information described in the law that most closely describes the personal information sold and shared, and provide the categories of third parties to whom the consumer's personal information was sold or shared.\\n\\n(2) Identify by category or categories the personal information of the consumer that the business disclosed for a business purpose during the applicable period of time by reference to the enumerated category or categories of personal information and sensitive personal information described in the law that most closely describes the personal information, and provide the categories of persons to whom the consumer’s personal information was disclosed for a business purpose.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) In responding to a consumer’s verified request to know categories of personal\\ninformation, categories of sources, and/or categories of third parties, a business shall\\nprovide an individualized response to the consumer as required by the CCPA.\\n\\n(2) It shall not refer the consumer to the businesses’ Information Practices  outlined in its privacy policy unless its response would be the same for all consumers and the privacy policy discloses all the information that is otherwise required to be in a response to a request to know such categories. \"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall use reasonable security measures when transmitting personal information to the consumer. \"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall provide all the personal information it has collected and maintains about the consumer on or after January 1, 2022, including beyond\\nthe 12-month period preceding the business’s receipt of the request.\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (g)\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"(1) The confirmation may be given in the same manner in which the request was received. For example, if the request is made over the phone, the confirmation may be given orally during the phone call. \\n\\n(2) If a business maintains a password-protected account with the consumer, it may comply with a request to know by using a secure self-service portal for consumers to access, view, and receive a portable copy of their personal information if the portal fully discloses the personal information that the consumer is entitled to under the CCPA and these regulations, uses reasonable data security controls, and complies with the verification requirements of the law.\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"A business shall identify the categories of personal information, categories of sources of personal information, and categories of third parties to whom a business sold or disclosed personal information, in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of the categories listed.\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (l)\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"yes\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"(1) A business that operates exclusively online and has a direct relationship with a consumer from whom it collects personal information shall only be required to provide an email address for submitting requests to know.\\n\\n(2) Businesses that do not operate exclusively online shall provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests.\\n\\n(a) One of those methods must be a toll-free telephone number. \\n\\n(b) If the business maintains an internet website, one of the methods for submitting these requests shall be through its website, such as through a webform. \\n\\n(c) Other methods for submitting but are not limited to a designated email address, a form submitted in person, and a form submitted through the mail. \\n\\n(3) A business shall consider the methods by which it primarily interacts with consumers when determining which methods to provide for submitting requests. \\n\\n(4) If a consumer submits a request in a manner that is not one of the designated methods of submission, or is deficient in some manner unrelated to the verification process, the business shall either: \\n\\n(a) Treat the request as if it had been submitted in accordance with the business’s designated manner, or\\n\\n(b) Provide the consumer with information on how to submit the request or remedy any deficiencies with the request, if applicable.\\n\\n(5) A consumer may use an authorized agent with a signed permission to submit the request on their behalf. \",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"CPRA Regulations § 7020 (a) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (b) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (c) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (e) , CPRA Regulations § 7002 (d) , CPRA Regulations § 7063\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"A covered entity is not obligated to provide the information required by a consumer's request to know to the same consumer more than twice in a 12-month period.\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": 2,\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.110 , US CPRA Section 1798.115 , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (b) \",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"(1) The requests must be verified by the covered entity to have been sent by the concerned consumers themselves or agents of the consumer authorised to act on their behalf or they can be rejected.\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(b)\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"The business must describe to the consumer the categories of records that may contain personal information that it did not search because it meets the conditions stated above. \",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (c)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"a business is not required to search for personal information if all of the following conditions are met:\\n\\n(a) The business does not maintain the personal information in a searchable or reasonably accessible format;\\n\\n(b) The business maintains the personal information solely for legal or compliance\\npurposes;\\n\\n(c) The business does not sell the personal information and does not use it for any\\ncommercial purpose; and\\n\\n(d) The business describes to the consumer the categories of records that may contain personal information that it did not search because it meets the conditions stated above. \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"General Limitations\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(q)(1)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The obligations imposed on businesses by an access request shall not apply to household data.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Household data exemption\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"If a business does not comply with an access request pursuant to this limitation, it shall notify the consumer that it is acting pursuant to this exception.\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) This limitation only applies if consumer access, possession, or control would jeopardize the validity and reliability of that educational standardized assessment or educational assessment.\\n\\n(2) “Educational standardized assessment or educational assessment” means a standardized or nonstandardized quiz, test, or other assessment used to evaluate students in or for entry to kindergarten and grades 1 to 12, inclusive, schools, postsecondary institutions, vocational programs, and postgraduate programs that are accredited by an accrediting agency or organization recognized by the State of California or the United States Department of Education, as well as certification and licensure examinations used to determine competency and eligibility to receive certification or licensure from a government agency or government certification body.\\n\\n(3) “Jeopardize the validity and reliability of that educational standardized assessment or educational assessment” means releasing information that would provide an advantage to the consumer who has submitted a verifiable consumer request or to another natural person.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(q)(2) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(q)(3)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The law does not require, in response to an access request, that a business disclose on educational standardized assessment or educational assessment or a consumer’s specific responses to the educational standardized assessment or educational assessment.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Educational Assessment Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"There must be an adverse impact to another natural persons rights or freedoms.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(k)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"(1) The rights afforded to consumers and the obligations imposed on the business in this law shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of other natural persons.\\n\\n(2) A verifiable consumer request shall not extend to personal information about the consumer that belongs to, or the business maintains on behalf of, another natural person. \\n\\n(3) A covered entity is under no legal requirement to seek out other persons that may have or claim to have rights to personal information, and a business is under no legal obligation under this law or any other provision of law to take any action under this law in the event of a dispute between or among persons claiming rights to personal information in the business’ possession.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Other Natural Persons Exception\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1798.110. , https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=1798.115.&nodeTreePath=8.4.47&lawCode=CIV , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/20221102_mod_text.pdf\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.110 , US CPRA Section 1798.115 , US CPRA Regulations § 7002 (z) , US CPRA Regulations § 7002 (ff) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (k)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"Consumers have a right to know:\\n\\n(1) Specific pieces of personal information that a business has collected about the consumer;\\n\\n(2) Categories of personal information it has collected about the consumer;\\n\\n(3) Categories of sources from which the personal information is collected;\\n\\n(4) Categories of personal information that the business sold or disclosed for a business purpose about the consumer;\\n\\n(5) Categories of third parties to whom the personal information was sold or disclosed for a business purpose; and \\n\\n(6) The business or commercial purpose for collecting or selling personal information. \",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to Know\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"The time period to provide the required information in a consumer's DSR request may be extended once by an additional 45 days if considered reasonably necessary by the covered entity, taking into account the complexity and number of the DSR requests.\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"The consumer who has requested the DSR, must be provided a notice of extension of time by the covered entity within the 45 day initial period along with reasons for the delay.\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"Businesses may take up to an additional 45 calendar days to respond to the consumer’s request, for a maximum total of 90\\ncalendar days \",\n                        \"response_time\": \"A covered entity has 10 days to confirm receipt of the consumer request and 45 days to respond to a consumer's DSR request.\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"yes\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"End of the first 45 days.\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"The 45-day period will begin on the day that the covered entity receives the request, regardless of\\ntime required to verify the request.\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": [\n                        {\n                            \"citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (d)\",\n                            \"details\": \"A business shall not disclose in response to a request to know a consumer’s Social Security\\nnumber, driver’s license number or other government-issued identification number, financial\\naccount number, any health insurance or medical identification number, an account password, security questions and answers, or unique biometric data generated from measurements or technical analysis of human characteristics. The business shall, however,inform the consumer with sufficient particularity that it has collected the type of information.\",\n                            \"type\": \"Certain Specific Pieces of Personal Information\"\n                        }\n                    ]\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToErasure\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"(1) No later than 10 business days after receiving a request a business shall confirm receipt of the request and provide information about how the business will process the request. The information provided shall describe in general the business’s verification process and when the consumer should expect a response, except in instances where the business has already granted or denied the request. \\n\\n(2) In responding to a request to delete, a business shall \\n\\n(a) inform the consumer whether or not it has complied with the consumer’s request.\\n\\n(b) it will maintain a record of the request as required by the law.\\n\\n(3) If a business claims that notifying some or all third parties about the consumer's request to delete would be impossible or would involve disproportionate effort, the business shall provide the consumer a detailed explanation that includes enough facts to give a consumer a meaningful understanding as to why the business cannot notify all third parties. The business shall not simply state that notifying all third parties is impossible or would require disproportionate effort. \\n\\n(4) In responding to a request to delete, a business may present the consumer with the choice to delete select portions of their personal information as long as a single option to delete all personal information is also offered. A business that provides consumers the ability to delete select categories of personal information (e.g., purchase history, browsing history, voice recordings) in other contexts, however, must inform consumers of their ability to do so and direct them to how they can do so. For example, a business may provide the consumer with a link to a support page or other resource that explains consumers’ data deletion options.\",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (b)(3) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (h)\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (c) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (d)\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall comply with a consumer’s request to delete their personal information by: \\n\\n(1) Permanently and completely erasing the personal information from its existing systems except archived or back-up systems, de-identifying the personal information or  aggregating the consumer information;\\n\\n(2) Notifying the business’s service providers or contractors to delete from their records the consumer’s personal information that they collected pursuant to their written contract with the business, or if enabled to do so by the service provider or contractor, the business shall delete the personal information that the service provider or contractor collected pursuant to their written contract with the business\\n\\n(3) Notifying all third parties to whom the business has sold or shared the personal\\ninformation to delete the consumer’s personal information unless this proves impossible or involves disproportionate effort.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A service provider or contractor shall, with respect to personal information that they collected pursuant to their written contract with the business and upon notification by the business, cooperate with the business in responding to a request to delete by:\\n\\n(a) Permanently and completely erasing the personal information from its existing systems except archived or back-up systems, deidentifying the personal information, or aggregating the consumer information, or enabling the business to do so;\\n\\n(b) To the extent that an exception applies to the deletion of personal information, deleting or enabling the business to delete the consumer’s personal information that is not\\nsubject to the exception and refraining from using the consumer’s personal information retained for any purpose other than the purpose provided for by that exception;\\n\\n(c) Notifying any of its own service providers or contractors to delete from their records in the same manner the consumer’s personal information that they collected pursuant to their written contract with the service provider or contractor; \\n\\n(d) Notifying any other service providers, contractors, or third parties that may have accessed personal information from or through the service provider or contractor, unless the information was accessed at the direction of the business, to delete the consumer’s personal information unless this proves impossible or involves disproportionate effort.\\n\\n(2) If a business, service provider, or contractor stores any personal information on archived or backup systems, it may delay compliance with the consumer’s request to delete, with respect to data stored on the archived or backup system, until the archived or backup system relating to that data is restored to an active system or is next accessed or used for a sale, disclosure, or commercial purpose.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business, service provider, contractor, or third party may retain a record of the request for the purpose of ensuring that the consumer’s personal information remains deleted from its records\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a)\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"The confirmation may be given in the same manner in which the request was received. For example, if the request is made over the phone, the confirmation may be given orally during the phone call. \",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"yes\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"(1) A business that operates exclusively online and has a direct relationship with a consumer from whom it collects personal information shall only be required to provide an email address for submitting requests to delete.\\n\\n(2) Businesses that do not operate exclusively online shall provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests.\\n\\n(a) One of those methods must be a toll-free telephone number. \\n\\n(b) If the business maintains an internet website, one of the methods for submitting these requests shall be through its website, such as through a webform. \\n\\n(c) Other methods for submitting but are not limited to a designated email address, a form submitted in person, and a form submitted through the mail. \\n\\n(3) A business shall consider the methods by which it primarily interacts with consumers when determining which methods to provide for submitting requests. \\n\\n(4) If a consumer submits a request in a manner that is not one of the designated methods of submission, or is deficient in some manner unrelated to the verification process, the business shall either: \\n\\n(a) Treat the request as if it had been submitted in accordance with the business’s designated manner, or\\n\\n(b) Provide the consumer with information on how to submit the request or remedy any deficiencies with the request, if applicable.\\n\\n(5) A business may use a two-step process for online requests to delete where the consumer must first, submit the request to delete and then second, separately confirm that they want their personal information deleted.\\n\\n(6) A consumer may use an authorized agent with a signed permission to submit the request on their behalf. \",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"CPRA Regulations § 7020 (a) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (b) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (c) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (d) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (e) , CPRA Regulations § 7002 (d) , CPRA Regulations § 7063\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.110 , US CPRA Section 1798.115 , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (b) \",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"(1) The requests must be verified by the covered entity to have been sent by the concerned consumers themselves or agents of the consumer authorised to act on their behalf or they can be rejected.\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"The limitation related to personal information being used for public or peer-reviewed scientific, historical, or statistical research only applies when the business's deletion of the information is likely to render impossible or seriously impair the ability to complete such research, if the consumer has provided informed consent.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.105(d)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"A business, or a service provider or contractor, acting pursuant to its contract with the business, another service provider, or another contractor, shall not be required to comply with a consumer's request to delete the consumer's personal information if it is reasonably necessary for the business service provider, or contractor to maintain the consumer's personal information in order to:\\n\\n(1) Complete the transaction for which the personal information was collected, fulfill the terms of a written warranty or product recall conducted in accordance with federal law, provide a good or service requested by the consumer, or reasonably anticipated by the consumer within the context of a business's ongoing business relationship with the consumer, or otherwise perform a contract between the business and the consumer.\\n\\n(2) Help to ensure security and integrity to the extent the use of the consumer's personal information is reasonably necessary and proportionate for those purposes.\\n\\n(3) Debug to identify and repair errors that impair existing intended functionality.\\n\\n(4) Exercise free speech, ensure the right of another consumer to exercise his/her right to free speech.\\n\\n(5) Comply with the California Electronic Communications Privacy Act pursuant to Chapter 3.6 (commencing with Section 1546) of Title 12 of Part 2 of the Penal Code.\\n\\n(6) Engage in public or peer-reviewed scientific, historical, or statistical research that conforms or adheres to all other applicable ethics and privacy laws.\\n\\n(7) To enable solely internal uses that are reasonably aligned with the expectations of the consumer based on the consumer's relationship with the business and compatible with the context in which the consumer provided the information.\\n\\n(8) Comply with a legal obligation.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"General Limitations\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) This limitation applies to the extent that the commercial credit reporting agency uses the business controller information solely to identify the relationship of a consumer to a business that the consumer owns or contact the consumer only in the consumer’s role as the owner, director, officer, or management employee of the business.\\n\\n(2) “Business controller information” means the name or names of the owner or owners, director, officer, or management employee of a business and the contact information, including a business title, for the owner or owners, director, officer, or management employee.\\n\\n(3) “Commercial credit reporting agency” has the meaning set forth in subdivision (b) of Section 1785.42.\\n\\n(4) \\\"Owner” means a natural person that meets one of the following:\\n\\n(a) Has ownership of, or the power to vote, more than 50 percent of the outstanding shares of any class of voting security of a business.\\n\\n(b) Has control in any manner over the election of a majority of the directors or of individuals exercising similar functions.\\n\\n(c) Has the power to exercise a controlling influence over the management of a company.\\n\\n(5) Director” means a natural person designated in the articles of incorporation of a business as director, or elected by the incorporators and natural persons designated, elected, or appointed by any other name or title to act as directors, and their successors.\\n\\n(6) “Officer” means a natural person elected or appointed by the board of directors of a business to manage the daily operations of a corporation, including a chief executive officer, president, secretary, or treasurer.\\n\\n(7) “Management employee” means a natural person whose name and contact information is reported to or collected by a commercial credit reporting agency as the primary manager of a business and used solely within the context of the natural person’s role as the primary manager of the business.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(o)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"Consumer's right to delete shall not apply to a commercial credit reporting agency’s collection, processing, sale, or disclosure of business controller information.\\n\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Business Controller Information Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"There must be an adverse impact to another natural persons rights or freedoms.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(k)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"(1) The rights afforded to consumers and the obligations imposed on the business in this law shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of other natural persons.\\n\\n(2) A verifiable consumer request shall not extend to personal information about the consumer that belongs to, or the business maintains on behalf of, another natural person. \\n\\n(3) A covered entity is under no legal requirement to seek out other persons that may have or claim to have rights to personal information, and a business is under no legal obligation under this law or any other provision of law to take any action under this law in the event of a dispute between or among persons claiming rights to personal information in the business’ possession.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Other Natural Persons Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(p)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The obligations imposed on businesses by a deletion request shall not apply to household data.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Household Data Exemption \"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"If a business does not comply with a request to delete due to this limitation, it shall notify the consumer that it is acting pursuant to this exception.\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(q)(1)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"This law does not require a business to comply with a verifiable consumer request to delete a consumer’s personal information to the extent the verifiable consumer request applies to a student’s grades, educational scores, or educational test results that the business holds on behalf of a local educational agency, as defined in subdivision (d) of Section 49073.1 of the Education Code, at which the student is currently enrolled. \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Educational Scores Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"The limitation applies only if:\\n\\n(1) The business has incurred significant expense in reliance on the consumer’s consent.\\n\\n(2) Compliance with the consumer’s request to delete the consumer’s personal information would not be commercially reasonable.\\n\\n(3) The business complies with the consumer’s request as soon as it is commercially reasonable to do so.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(r)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The right to delete shall not apply to a business’ use, disclosure, or sale of particular pieces of a consumer’s personal information if the consumer has consented to the business’ use, disclosure, or sale of that information to produce a physical item, including a school yearbook containing the consumer’s photograph.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Production of Physical Item Exception\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.105 , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (y) , US Regulations § 7001 (ee)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"A consumer shall have the right to request that a covered entity delete any personal information about the consumer which it has collected from the consumer.\",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to Delete\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"The time period to comply with a consumer's DSR request may be extended once by an additional 45 days if considered reasonably necessary by the covered entity, taking into account the complexity and number of the DSR requests.\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"The consumer who has requested the DSR, must be provided a notice of extension of time by the covered entity within the 45 day initial period along with reasons for the delay.\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"Businesses may take up to an additional 45 calendar days to respond to the consumer’s request, for a maximum total of 90\\ncalendar days \",\n                        \"response_time\": \"A covered entity has 10 days to confirm receipt of the consumer request and 45 days to respond to a consumer's DSR request.\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"yes\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"End of the first 45 days.\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"The 45-day period will begin on the day that the covered entity receives the request, regardless of\\ntime required to verify the request.\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": []\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToRectification\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"(1) No later than 10 business days after receiving a request a business shall confirm receipt of the request and provide information about how the business will process the request. The information provided shall describe in general the business’s verification process and when the consumer should expect a response, except in instances where the business has already granted or denied the request. \\n\\n(2) In responding to a request to correct, a business shall inform the consumer whether it has complied with the consumer's request to correct or not.\\n\\n(3) Where the business is not the source of the information that the consumer contends is\\ninaccurate, in addition to processing the consumer’s request, the business may provide the consumer with the name of the source from which the business received the alleged\\ninaccurate information. \\n\\n(3) Upon request, a business shall disclose a specific pieces of personal information that\\nthe business maintains and has collected about the consumer to allow the consumer to\\nconfirm that the business has corrected the inaccurate information that was the subject of the consumer’s request to correct. \\n\\n(a) This disclosure shall not be considered a response to a request to know that is counted towards the limitation of two requests within a 12-month period of consumers' right to access under this law. \",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (f) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (i) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (j)\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.106(c) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (b) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (c) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (d) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (e) , CPRA Regulations § 7023 (k)\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business that receives a verifiable consumer request to correct inaccurate personal information shall use commercially reasonable efforts to correct the inaccurate personal information.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) In determining the accuracy of the personal information that is the subject of a consumer’s request to correct, the business shall consider the totality of the circumstances relating to the contested personal information. \\n\\n(2) Considering the totality of the circumstances includes, but is not limited to, considering:\\n\\n(a) The nature of the personal information (e.g., whether it is objective, subjective,\\nunstructured, sensitive, etc.).\\n\\n(b) How the business obtained the contested information.\\n\\n(c) Documentation relating to the accuracy of the information whether provided by\\nthe consumer, the business, or another source. \\n\\n(3) If the business is not the source of the personal information and has no documentation in support of the accuracy of the information, the consumer’s assertion of inaccuracy may be sufficient to establish that the personal information is inaccurate.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A business that complies with a consumer’s request to correct shall correct the personal information at issue on its existing systems.\\n\\n(2) The business shall also instruct all service providers and contractors that maintain the personal information at issue pursuant to their written contract\\nwith the business to make the necessary corrections in their respective systems.\\n\\n(a) Service providers and contractors shall comply with the business’s instructions to correct the personal information or enable the business to make the corrections.\\n\\n(b) If a business, service provider, or contractor stores any personal\\ninformation that is the subject of the request to correct on archived or backup systems, it may delay compliance with the consumer’s request to correct, with respect to data stored on the archived or backup system, until the archived or backup system relating to that data is restored to an active system or is next accessed or used.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A business shall accept, review, and consider any documentation that the consumer provides in connection with their right to correct whether provided voluntarily or as required by the business.\\n\\n(2) A business may require the consumer to provide documentation if necessary to rebut its own documentation that the personal information is accurate. \\n\\n(3) In determining the necessity of the documentation requested, the business shall consider the following:  \\n\\n(a) The nature of the personal information at issue (e.g., whether it is objective,\\nsubjective, unstructured, sensitive, etc.).\\n\\n(b) The nature of the documentation upon which the business considers the personal\\ninformation to be accurate (e.g., whether the documentation is from a trusted\\nsource, whether the documentation is verifiable, etc.).\\n\\n(c) The purpose for which the business collects, maintains, or uses the personal\\ninformation. For example, if the personal information is essential to the functioning of the business, the business may require more documentation.\\n\\n(d) The impact on the consumer - if the inaccurate personal information has a negative impact on the consumer -the business may require less documentation.\\n\\n(4) Any documentation provided by the consumer in connection with their request to correct shall only be used and/or maintained by the business for the purpose of correcting the consumer’s personal information and to comply with the record-keeping obligations under the law.\\n\\n(5) The business shall implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices in maintaining any documentation relating to the consumer’s request to correct. \"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business may delete the contested personal information as an alternative to correcting the information if the deletion of the personal information does not negatively impact the consumer, or the consumer consents to the deletion. \"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business, service provider and contractor have to to ensure that personal information that is the subject of a request to correct remains corrected and is not subsequently overridden by inaccurate personal information from another source.\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (a)\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"The confirmation may be given in the same manner in which the request was received. For example, if the request is made over the phone, the confirmation may be given orally during the phone call. \",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"yes\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"(1) A business that operates exclusively online and has a direct relationship with a consumer from whom it collects personal information shall only be required to provide an email address for submitting requests to know.\\n\\n(2) Businesses that do not operate exclusively online shall provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests.\\n\\n(a) One of those methods must be a toll-free telephone number. \\n\\n(b) If the business maintains an internet website, one of the methods for submitting these requests shall be through its website, such as through a webform. \\n\\n(c) Other methods for submitting but are not limited to a designated email address, a form submitted in person, and a form submitted through the mail. \\n\\n(3) A business shall consider the methods by which it primarily interacts with consumers when determining which methods to provide for submitting requests. \\n\\n(4) If a consumer submits a request in a manner that is not one of the designated methods of submission, or is deficient in some manner unrelated to the verification process, the business shall either: \\n\\n(a) Treat the request as if it had been submitted in accordance with the business’s designated manner, or\\n\\n(b) Provide the consumer with information on how to submit the request or remedy any deficiencies with the request, if applicable.\\n\\n(5) A consumer may use an authorized agent with a signed permission to submit the request on their behalf. \",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"CPRA Regulations § 7020 (a) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (b) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (c) , CPRA Regulations § 7020 (e) , CPRA Regulations § 7001 (d) , CPRA Regulations § 7063\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.110 , US CPRA Section 1798.115 , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (b) \",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"(1) The requests must be verified by the covered entity to have been sent by the concerned consumers themselves or agents of the consumer authorised to act on their behalf or they can be rejected.\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"There must be an adverse impact to another natural persons rights or freedoms.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(k)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"(1) The rights afforded to consumers and the obligations imposed on the business in this law shall not adversely affect the rights and freedoms of other natural persons.\\n\\n(2) A verifiable consumer request shall not extend to personal information about the consumer that belongs to, or the business maintains on behalf of, another natural person. \\n\\n(3) A covered entity is under no legal requirement to seek out other persons that may have or claim to have rights to personal information, and a business is under no legal obligation under this law or any other provision of law to take any action under this law in the event of a dispute between or among persons claiming rights to personal information in the business’ possession.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Other Natural Persons Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(p)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The obligations imposed on businesses by an access request shall not apply to household data.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Household Data Exemption\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.106 , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (x) , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (ff)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"A consumer shall have the right to request a business to\\ncorrect inaccurate personal information that it maintains about the consumer.\",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to Correct\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"The time period to provide the required information in a consumer's DSR request may be extended once by an additional 45 days if considered reasonably necessary by the covered entity, taking into account the complexity and number of the DSR requests.\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"The consumer who has requested the DSR, must be provided a notice of extension of time by the covered entity within the 45 day initial period along with reasons for the delay.\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021 (b)\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"Businesses may take up to an additional 45 calendar days to respond to the consumer’s request, for a maximum total of 90\\ncalendar days \",\n                        \"response_time\": \"A covered entity has 10 days to confirm receipt of the consumer request and 45 days to respond to a consumer's DSR request.\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"yes\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"45 days\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"End of the first 45 days.\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"The 45-day period will begin on the day that the covered entity receives the request, regardless of\\ntime required to verify the request.\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": [\n                        {\n                            \"citation\": \"CPRA § 7023 (j)\",\n                            \"details\": \"With regard to a request for correction to a consumer’s Social Security number, driver’s license number or other government-issued identification number, financial account number, any health insurance or medical identification number, an account password, security questions and answers, or unique biometric data generated from measurements or technical analysis of human characteristics - a business shall not disclose this information, but may provide a way to confirm that the personal information it maintains is the same as what the consumer has provided.\",\n                            \"type\": \"Certain Specific Pieces of Information\"\n                        }\n                    ]\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToNotSell\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"(1) In responding to a request to opt-out of sale/sharing, a business may present the consumer with the choice to opt-out of the sale or sharing of personal information for certain uses as long as a single option o opt-out of the sale or sharing of all personal information is also offered.\\n\\n(2) A business that responds to a request to opt-out of sale/sharing by informing the consumer of a charge for the use of any product or service shall provide the consumer with a Notice of Financial Incentive.\",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (h) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (i)\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.120(d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (k) , US CPRA Regulations § 7028\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business that has received direction from a consumer not to sell or share their personal information shall be prohibited from selling or sharing the consumer's personal information after its receipt of the consumer's direction, unless the consumer subsequently provides consent.\\n\\n(2) A business shall comply with a request to opt-out of sale/sharing by ceasing to sell to and/or share with third parties the consumer’s personal information.\\n\\n(3) Notifying all third parties to whom the business has sold or shared the consumer’s personal information, after the consumer submits the request to opt-out of sale/sharing and before the business complies with that request, that the consumer has made a request to opt-out of sale/sharing and directing them to comply with the consumer’s request and forward the request to any other person to whom the third party  has made the personal information available during that time period.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A business shall wait at least 12 months from the date the consumer’s request before asking a consumer who has opted out of the sale or sharing of their personal information to consent to the sale or sharing of their personal\\ninformation. \\n\\n(2) If a consumer who has opted-out of the sale or sharing of their personal information initiates a transaction or attempts to use a product or service that requires the sale or sharing of their personal information, the business may inform the consumer that the transaction, product, or service requires the sale of their personal information and provide instructions on how the consumer can provide consent to opt-in to the sale or sharing of their personal information. \"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"US CPRA § 7026 (d)\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"A business may provide a means by which the consumer can confirm that their request to opt-out of sale/sharing has been processed by the business. For example, the business may display on its website “Consumer Opted Out of Sale/Sharing” or display through a toggle or radio button that the consumer has opted out.\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"yes\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"(1) A business that sells or shares personal information shall provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing.\\n\\n(2) A business shall consider the methods by which it interacts with consumers, the manner in which the business collects the personal information it makes available to third parties, available technology, and ease of use by the consumer when determining which methods consumers may use to submit requests to opt-out of sale/sharing. \\n\\n(3) At least one method offered shall reflect the manner in which the business primarily interacts with the consumer.\\n\\n(a) A business that collects personal information from consumers online, shall at a minimum:\\n\\n(i) allow consumers to submit requests to opt-out of sale/sharing through an opt-out preference signal - a signal that is sent by a platform, technology, or mechanism, on behalf of the consumer, that communicates the consumer choice to opt-out of the sale and sharing of personal information.\\n\\n(ii) offer at least one of the following methods:\\n\\n(A) an interactive form accessible via the “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” link on the homepage of the business's website.\\n\\n(B) An Alternative Opt-out Link - which is a single, clearly-labeled link that allows consumers to easily\\nexercise both their right to opt-out of sale/sharing and right to limit, instead of posting the\\ntwo separate “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” and “Limit the Use of My\\nSensitive Personal Information” links, or\\n\\n(C) The business’s privacy policy if the business processes an opt-out preference signal in a frictionless manner. \\n\\n(b) A business that interacts with consumers in person and online may provide an inperson method for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing in addition to the opt-out preference signal. \\n\\n(c) Other methods for submitting requests to opt-out of the sale/sharing include, but are\\nnot limited to:\\n\\n(i) a toll-free phone number, \\n\\n(ii) a designated email address, \\n\\n(iii) a form submitted in person, and \\n\\n(iv) a form submitted through the mail. \\n\\n(d) A notification or tool regarding cookies, such as a cookie banner or cookie controls, is not by itself an acceptable method for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing because cookies concern the collection of personal information and not the sale or sharing of personal information. An acceptable method for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing must address the sale and sharing of personal information. \\n\\n(4) A business’s methods for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing shall be easy for consumers to execute and shall require minimal steps. \\n\\n(5) A business shall not require a consumer submitting a request to opt-out of sale/sharing to\\ncreate an account or provide additional information beyond what is necessary to direct the business not to sell or share the consumer’s personal information. \\n\\n(6) A business shall not require a verifiable consumer request for a request to opt-out of sale/sharing. \\n\\n(7) A consumer may use an authorized agent to submit a request to opt-out of sale/sharing\\non the consumer’s behalf if the consumer provides the authorized agent written permission\\nsigned by the consumer.\\n\\n(a) A business may deny a request from an authorized agent if the agent does not provide to the business the consumer’s signed permission\\ndemonstrating that they have been authorized by the consumer to act on the consumer’s\\nbehalf.\\n\\n(b) The requirement to obtain and provide written permission from the consumer does not apply to requests made by an opt-out preference signal.\",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (u) , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (j)\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.120 , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (j) \",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"(1) This right only applies if the business sells or shares the personal information it collects about consumers.\\n\\n(2) A request to opt-out need not be a verifiable consumer request but it may also not be made fraudulently.\\n\\n(a) A business may ask the consumer for information\\nnecessary to complete the request, such as information necessary to identify the consumer whose information shall cease to be sold or shared by the business. \\n\\n(b) However, to the extent that the business can comply with a request to opt-out of sale/sharing without additional\\ninformation, it shall do so. \\n\\n(3) An authorized agent acting on behalf of a consumer to exercise the right must be able to provide written permission signed by the consumer.\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"If a third party materially alters how it uses or shares the personal information of a consumer in a manner that is materially inconsistent with the promises made at the time of collection, it shall provide prior notice of the new or changed practice to the consumer. The notice shall be sufficiently prominent and robust to ensure that existing consumers can easily exercise their choices consistently with this title.\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) This limitation applies provided that the personal information obtained through the merger is used or shared consistently with this law.\\n\\n(2) This limitation does not authorize a business to make material, retroactive privacy policy changes or make other changes in their privacy policy in a manner that would violate the Unfair and Deceptive Practices Act (Chapter 5 (commencing with Section 17200) of Part 2 of Division 7 of the Business and Professions Code).\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.140(ad) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(ah) \",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The business transfers to a third party the personal information of a consumer as an asset that is part of a merger, acquisition, bankruptcy, or other transaction in which the third party assumes control of all or part of the business, is not considered the sale or sharing of personal information. \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Merger Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"The limitation applies only if:\\n\\n(1) The business has incurred significant expense in reliance on the consumer’s consent.\\n\\n(2) Compliance with the consumer’s request to delete the consumer’s personal information would not be commercially reasonable.\\n\\n(3) The business complies with the consumer’s request as soon as it is commercially reasonable to do so.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(r)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"The right to opt-out of the sale or sharing of personal information shall not apply to a business’ use, disclosure, or sale of particular pieces of a consumer’s personal information if the consumer has consented to the business’ use, disclosure, or sale of that information to produce a physical item, including a school yearbook containing the consumer’s photograph.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Production of a Physical Item Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) This limitation only applies if the vehicle or ownership information is shared for the purpose of effectuating, or in anticipation of effectuating, a vehicle repair covered by a vehicle warranty or a recall conducted pursuant to Sections 30118 to 30120, inclusive, of Title 49 of the United States Code, provided that the new motor vehicle dealer or vehicle manufacturer with which that vehicle information or ownership information is shared does not sell, share, or use that information for any other purpose.\\n\\n(2) For purposes of this limitation:\\n\\n(a) “Vehicle information” means the vehicle information number, make, model, year, and odometer reading.\\n\\n(b) “Ownership information” means the name or names of the registered owner or owners and the contact information for the owner or owners.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA 1798.145(g)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"Consumer's right to not sell or share personal information shall not apply to vehicle information or ownership information retained or shared between a new motor vehicle dealer, as defined in Section 426 of the Vehicle Code, and the vehicle’s manufacturer, as defined in Section 672 of the Vehicle Code.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Vehicle Repair under Warranty/Recall Exception\"\n                        },\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"(1) This limitation applies to the extent that the commercial credit reporting agency uses the business controller information solely to identify the relationship of a consumer to a business that the consumer owns or contact the consumer only in the consumer’s role as the owner, director, officer, or management employee of the business.\\n\\n(2) “Business controller information” means the name or names of the owner or owners, director, officer, or management employee of a business and the contact information, including a business title, for the owner or owners, director, officer, or management employee.\\n\\n(3) “Commercial credit reporting agency” has the meaning set forth in subdivision (b) of Section 1785.42.\\n\\n(4) \\\"Owner” means a natural person that meets one of the following:\\n\\n(a) Has ownership of, or the power to vote, more than 50 percent of the outstanding shares of any class of voting security of a business.\\n\\n(b) Has control in any manner over the election of a majority of the directors or of individuals exercising similar functions.\\n\\n(c) Has the power to exercise a controlling influence over the management of a company.\\n\\n(5) Director” means a natural person designated in the articles of incorporation of a business as director, or elected by the incorporators and natural persons designated, elected, or appointed by any other name or title to act as directors, and their successors.\\n\\n(6) “Officer” means a natural person elected or appointed by the board of directors of a business to manage the daily operations of a corporation, including a chief executive officer, president, secretary, or treasurer.\\n\\n(7) “Management employee” means a natural person whose name and contact information is reported to or collected by a commercial credit reporting agency as the primary manager of a business and used solely within the context of the natural person’s role as the primary manager of the business.\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(o)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"Consumer's right to opt-out of sale or sharing of personal information shall not apply to a commercial credit reporting agency’s collection, processing, sale, or disclosure of business controller information.\",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"Business Controller Information Exception\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.120 , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (cc) , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (hh) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(ad) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(ah) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(ai) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(k)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"(1) A consumer shall have the right to direct a business that sells or shares personal information about the consumer to third parties\\nto neither sell nor share the consumer’s personal information to third parties.\\n\\n(2) (a) \\\"Sell,\\\" \\\"selling,\\\" \\\"sale,\\\" or \\\"sold,'' means selling, renting, releasing, disclosing, disseminating, making available, transferring, or otherwise communicating orally, In writing, or by electronic or other means, a consumer's personal information by the business to a third party for monetary or other valuable consideration.\\n\\n(b) A business does not sell personal information when:\\n\\n(i) A consumer uses or directs the business to: \\n\\n(A) intentionally disclose personal information or \\n\\n(B) intentionally interact with one or more third parties;\\n\\n(ii) The business uses or shares an identifier for a consumer who has opted out of the sale of the consumer's personal information or limited the use of the consumer's sensitive personal information for the purposes of alerting persons that the consumer has opted out of the sale of the consumer's personal information or limited the use of the consumer's sensitive personal information;\\n\\n(3) (a) “Share,” “shared,” or “sharing” means sharing, renting, releasing, disclosing, disseminating, making available, transferring, or otherwise communicating orally, in writing, or by electronic or other means, a consumer’s personal information by the business to a third party for cross-context behavioral advertising, whether or not for monetary or other valuable consideration, including transactions between a business and a third party for cross-context behavioral advertising for the benefit of a business in which no money is exchanged.\\n\\n(b) A business does not share personal information when:\\n\\n(i) A consumer uses or directs the business to intentionally disclose personal information or intentionally interact with one or more third parties.\\n\\n(ii) The business uses or shares an identifier for a consumer who has opted out of the sharing of the consumer’s personal information or limited the use of the consumer’s sensitive personal information for the purposes of alerting persons that the consumer has opted out of the sharing of the consumer’s personal information or limited the use of the consumer’s sensitive personal information.\\n\\n(3) “Cross-context behavioral advertising” means the targeting of advertising to a consumer based on the consumer’s personal information obtained from the consumer’s activity across businesses, distinctly-branded websites, applications, or services, other than the business, distinctly-branded website, application, or service with which the consumer intentionally interacts.\\n\\n(4) “Third party” means a person who is not any of the following:\\n\\n(a) The business with whom the consumer intentionally interacts and that collects personal information from the consumer as part of the consumer’s current interaction with the business under this law.\\n\\n(b) A service provider to the business.\\n\\n(c) A contractor.\",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to opt-out of sale and sharing\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (f)(1)\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time\": \"As soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 15 business days.\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"no\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"15 days\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"From the date the business receives the request.\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": []\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"other\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"(1) In responding to a request to limit use of sensitive personal information, a business may present the consumer with the choice to allow specific uses for the sensitive personal information as long as a single option of limit the use of the personal information is also offered.\\n\\n(2) A business that responds to a request to limit by informing the consumer of a charge for the use of any product or service shall shall provide the consumer with a Notice of Financial Incentive.\",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \" US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (i) ,  US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (k)\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.121 , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (g) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (l) \",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall comply with a request to limit by:\\n\\n(1) Ceasing to use and disclose the consumer’s sensitive personal information for any other purpose other than the exempt purposes.\\n\\n(2) Notifying all the business’s service providers or contractors that use or disclose the\\nconsumer’s sensitive personal information for purposes other than the exempt purposes that the consumer has made a request to limit and instructing them to comply with the consumer’s request to limit within the same time frame.\\n\\n(3) Notifying all third parties to whom the business has disclosed or made available the consumer’s sensitive personal for purposes other than the exempt purposes, after the consumer submitted their request and before the business complied with that request, that the consumer has made a request to limit and direct them to: \\n\\n(a) to comply with the consumer’s request, and\\n\\n(b) to forward the consumer's request to any other person with whom the person has disclosed or shared the sensitive personal information during that time period.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall wait at least 12 months from the date the consumer’s request to limit the use of sensitive personal information is received before asking a consumer who has exercised their right to consent to the use or disclosure of their sensitive personal information for purposes other than the exempt purposes.\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \" US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (h)\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"A business may provide a means by which the consumer can confirm that their request to limit the use of sensitive personal information has been processed by the business. For example, the business may display through a toggle or radio button that the consumer has limited the business’s use and sale of their sensitive personal information.\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"yes\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"(1) A business that uses or discloses sensitive personal information beyond exempt purposes shall provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests to opt-out of sale/sharing.\\n\\n(2) A business shall consider the methods by which it interacts with consumers, the manner in which the business collects the sensitive personal information that it uses or discloses beyond the exempt purposes, available technology, and ease of use by the consumer when determining which methods consumers may use to submit requests to limit use of sensitive personal information.\\n\\n(3) At least one method offered shall reflect the manner in which the business primarily interacts with the consumer.\\n\\n(a) A business that collects personal information from consumers online, shall at a minimum offer an interactive form accessible via the “Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information” link or an Alternative Opt-out Link - which is a single, clearly-labeled link that allows consumers to easily exercise both their right to opt-out of sale/sharing and right to limit, instead of posting the two separate “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” and “Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information” links, or\\n\\n(b) A business that interacts with consumers in person and online may provide an inperson method for submitting requests to limit use of sensitive personal information in addition to the online form.\\n\\n(c) Other methods for submitting requests to limit the use of sensitive personal information include, but are not limited to:\\n\\n(i) a toll-free phone number, \\n\\n(ii) a designated email address, \\n\\n(iii) a form submitted in person, and \\n\\n(iv) a form submitted through the mail. \\n\\n(d) A notification or tool regarding cookies, such as a cookie banner or cookie controls, is not by itself an acceptable method for submitting requests to limit use of sensitive personal information because cookies concern the collection of personal information and not the use and disclosures of sensitive personal information. An acceptable method for submitting requests to limit use of sensitive personal information must address the use and disclosure of sensitive personal information. \\n\\n(4) A business’s methods for submitting requests to limit the use of sensitive personal information shall be easy for consumers to execute and shall require minimal steps. \\n\\n(5) A business shall not require a consumer submitting a request to limit the use of sensitive personal information to create an account or provide additional information beyond what is necessary to limit the\\nuse or disclosure of the consumer’s sensitive personal information. \\n\\n(6) A business shall not require a verifiable consumer request for a request to limit the use of sensitive personal information.\\n\\n(7) (a) A consumer may use an authorized agent to submit a request to limit the use of sensitive personal information on the consumer’s behalf if the consumer provides the authorized agent written permission signed by the consumer.\\n\\n(b) A business may deny a request from an authorized agent if the agent does not provide to the business the consumer’s signed permission demonstrating that they have been authorized by the consumer to act on the consumer’s behalf.\",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (c) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (j)\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.121 , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (j) \",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"(1) This right only applies if the business uses or discloses the sensitive personal information it collects about consumers for any purposes beyond the exempt purposes.\\n\\n(2) A request to limit use of sensitive personal information need not be a verifiable consumer request but it may also not be made fraudulently.\\n\\n(a) A business may ask the consumer for information\\nnecessary to complete the request, such as information necessary to identify the consumer whose sensitive personal information shall cease to be used or disclosed beyond the exempt purposes. \\n\\n(b) However, to the extent that the business can comply with a request to limit use of sensitive personal information without additional information, it shall do so. \\n\\n(3) An authorized agent acting on behalf of a consumer to exercise the right must be able to provide written permission signed by the consumer.\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.121(d) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (m)(a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (m)(8)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"Sensitive personal information that is collected or processed without the purpose of inferring characteristics about a consumer is not subject to requests to limit.  \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"General Limitation\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1798.121. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/20221102_mod_text.pdf\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.121 , US CPRA Regulations § 7001(aa) , US CPRA Regulations § 7001 (gg) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027(m)\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"A consumer shall have the right, at any time, to direct a business that collects sensitive personal information about the consumer to limit its use and disclosure of the consumer's sensitive personal information to only that use which is necessary: \\n\\n(1) to perform the services or provide the goods reasonably expected by an average consumer who requests such goods or services;\\n\\n(2) to perform the following services:\\n\\n(a) (i) Helping to ensure security and integrity to the extent the use of the consumer’s personal information is reasonably necessary and proportionate for these purposes.\\n\\n(ii) To prevent, detect, and investigate security incidents that compromise the availability, authenticity, integrity, and or confidentiality of stored or transmitted personal information,\\n\\n(b) Short-term, transient use, including, but not limited to, non-personalized advertising shown as part of a consumer’s current interaction with the business, provided that the consumer’s personal information is not disclosed to another third party and is not used to build a profile about the consumer or otherwise alter the consumer’s experience outside the current interaction with the business.\\n\\n(c)  Performing services on behalf of the business, including maintaining or servicing accounts, providing customer service, processing or fulfilling orders and transactions, verifying customer information, processing payments, providing financing, providing analytic services, providing storage, or providing similar services on behalf of the business.\\n\\n(d) Undertaking activities to verify or maintain the quality or safety of a service or device that is owned, manufactured, manufactured for, or controlled by the business, and to improve, upgrade, or enhance the service or device that is owned, manufactured, manufactured for, or controlled by the business.\\n\\n(e) To resist malicious, deceptive, fraudulent, or illegal actions directed at the business and to prosecute those responsible for those actions.\\n\\n(f) To ensure the physical safety of natural persons.\",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Right to Limit Use of Sensitive Personal Information\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (g)(1)\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time\": \"As soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 15 business days.\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"no\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"15 days\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"From the date the business receives the request.\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": []\n                },\n                {\n                    \"category\": \"rightToNonDiscrimination\",\n                    \"data_controller_obligations\": {\n                        \"communication_content\": \"\",\n                        \"communication_content_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"obligation_citation\": \"CPRA Section 1798.125 , US CPRA Regulations § 7080 , US CPRA Regulations § 7081\",\n                        \"obligations\": [\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"(1) A business shall not discriminate against a consumer because the consumer exercised any of the consumer's rights under this law, Including, but not limited to, by:\\n\\n(1) Denying goods or services to the consumer.\\n\\n(2) Charging different prices or rates for goods or services, including through the use of discounts or other benefits or imposing penalties.\\n\\n(3) Providing a different level or quality of goods or services to the consumer.\\n\\n(4) Suggesting that the consumer will receive a different price or rate for goods or services or a different level or quality of goods or services.\\n\\n(5) Retaliating against an employee, applicant for employment, ar independent contractor.\\n\\n(2) A business may offer price or service difference that is nondiscriminatory. A price or service difference is non-discriminatory if it is reasonably related to the value of the consumer’s data.\\n\\n(a) If a business is unable to calculate a good-faith estimate of the value of the consumer’s data or cannot show that the price or service difference is reasonably related to the value of the consumer’s data, that business shall not offer it.\\n\\n(b) A business offering a price or service difference shall use and document a reasonable and good faith method for calculating the value of the consumer’s data. \\n\\n(i) The business shall consider one or more of the following:\\n\\n(A) The marginal value to the business of the sale, collection, or deletion of a consumer’s data.\\n\\n(B) The average value to the business of the sale, collection, or deletion of a consumer’s data.\\n\\n(C) The aggregate value to the business of the sale, collection, or deletion of consumers’ data divided by the total number of consumers.\\n\\n(D) Revenue generated by the business from sale, collection, or retention of consumers’ personal information.\\n\\n(E) Expenses related to the sale, collection, or retention of consumers’ personal information. \\n\\n(F) Expenses related to the offer, provision, or imposition of any financial incentive or price or service difference.\\n\\n(G) Profit generated by the business from sale, collection, or retention of consumers’ personal information.\\n\\n(H) Any other practical and reasonably reliable method of calculation used in good faith.\\n\\n(ii) For the purpose of calculating the value of consumer data, a business may consider the value to the business of the data of all natural persons in the United States and not just consumers. \"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall notify consumers of any financial incentive or price or service difference it offers through the notice of financial incentive.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business shall not use financial incentive practices that are unjust, unreasonable, coercive, or usurious in nature.\"\n                            },\n                            {\n                                \"obligation\": \"A business may enter a consumer into a financial incentive program only if the consumer gives the business prior opt-in consent  that clearly describes the material terms of the financial incentive program, and which may be revoked by the consumer at any time. If a consumer refuses to provide opt-In consent, then the business shall wait for at least 12 months before next requesting that the consumer provide opt-in consent,\"\n                            }\n                        ],\n                        \"response_form\": \"\",\n                        \"response_form_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_method_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_methods\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement\": \"\",\n                        \"transparent_communication_requirement_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr\": {\n                        \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                        \"can_exercise_as_dsr\": \"no\",\n                        \"make_request_methods\": \"\",\n                        \"make_request_methods_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times\": \"\",\n                        \"max_right_exercise_times_yearly\": \"\",\n                        \"right_circumstances_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"right_exercise_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"right_exercise_limits_citation\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"dsr_enforcement\": {\n                        \"circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcement_time_period_details\": \"\",\n                        \"enforcing_body_name\": \"Regulatory Body\",\n                        \"process\": \"\",\n                        \"time_period\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"limitations\": [\n                        {\n                            \"additional_limitation_obligations\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7080 (g)\",\n                            \"limitation_exemption_citation\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_exemptions\": \"\",\n                            \"limitation_overview\": \"A price or service difference that is the direct result of compliance with a state or federal law shall not be considered discriminatory. \",\n                            \"limitation_type\": \"State or Federal Law Exception\"\n                        }\n                    ],\n                    \"name\": {\n                        \"link\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                        \"right_citation\": \"CPRA Section 1798.125\",\n                        \"right_meaning\": \"(1) A consumer has a right to not be discriminated against for exercising their opt-out or any other rights provided under this law. \\n\\n(2) Consumers have right to be notified about financial incentives for allowing the collection, use, selling or sharing of their personal information and sensitive personal information and be offered a price or service difference if it is reasonably related to the value of the consumer’s data.\",\n                        \"right_name\": \"Consumers' Right of No Retaliation\"\n                    },\n                    \"response\": {\n                        \"citation\": \"\",\n                        \"citation_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_circumstances\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                        \"extension_time_citation\": \"\",\n                        \"response_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time\": \"\",\n                        \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_extension_time\": \"\",\n                        \"specify_response_time\": \"\",\n                        \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"\",\n                        \"when_time_period_commence\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"special_categories\": []\n                }\n            ],\n            \"documentation\": {\n                \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.199.40(b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7101\",\n                \"details\": \"(1) A business shall maintain records of consumer requests made pursuant to this law and how it responded to the requests for at least 24 months. The business shall implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices in maintaining these records.\\n\\n(2) A covered entity that knows or reasonably should know that it, alone or in combination, buys, receives for the covered entity’s commercial purposes, sells, or shares for commercial purposes the personal information of 10,000,000 or more consumers in a calendar year shall: \\n\\n(a) Compile the following metrics for the previous calendar year:\\n\\n(i) The number of requests to know that the business received, complied with in whole or in part, and denied;\\n\\n(ii) The number of requests to delete that the business received, complied with in whole or in part, and denied;\\n\\n(iii) The number of requests to opt-out that the business received, complied with in whole or in part, and denied; and\\n\\n(iv) The median or mean number of days within which the business substantive\\n\\n(b) Establish, document, and comply with a training policy to ensure that all individuals responsible for handling consumer requests made under this law or the covered entity’s compliance with this law are informed of all the requirements of this law and its associated regulations. \\n\\n(4) A covered entity shall document the verification processes it uses and verifications of consumer requests it complies with or denies. \\n\\n(5) A business’s maintenance of this information, where that information is not used for any other purpose, does not, taken alone, violate this law or these regulations.\\n\\n(6) Information maintained for record-keeping purposes shall not be used for any other purpose except as reasonably necessary for the business to review and modify its processes for compliance with this law and its associated regulations. Information maintained for recordkeeping purposes shall not be shared with any third party except as necessary to comply with a legal obligation.\\n\\n(7) Other than the record requirements described in the law, a business is not required to retain any other personal information solely for the purpose of fulfilling a consumer request made under this law\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                \"methods\": \"The records may be maintained in a ticket or log format provided that the ticket or log includes the date of request, nature of request, manner in which the request was made, the date of the covered entity’s response, the nature of the response, and the basis for the denial of the request if the request is denied in whole or in part. \"\n            },\n            \"dsr_enforcement\": [],\n            \"fees\": {\n                \"amount\": \"\",\n                \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(2) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(i)(3) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (i) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (k) \",\n                \"details\": \"(1) Covered entities must respond to DSR requests from consumers free of charge apart from in certain circumstances, in which case, a covered entity may charge a reasonable fee, taking into account the administrative costs of providing the information or communication or taking the action requested. \\n\\n(2) A business shall not require the consumer or the consumer’s authorized agent to pay a fee for the verification of their request to delete, request to correct, or request to know and must compensate consumers for any cost of notarization if it is required by the business for purposes of verifications.\\n\\n(3) A business’s charging of a reasonable fee for responding to manifestly unfounded or excessive or repetitive DSR requests shall not be considered a financial incentive under this law\",\n                \"fees_chargable_circumstances\": \"Covered entities may charge a reasonable fee if DSR requests from a consumer are manifestly unfounded or excessive, in particular because of their repetitive character. The covered entity shall bear the burden of demonstrating that any verified consumer request is manifestly unfounded or excessive.\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                \"maximum_account\": \"\"\n            },\n            \"general_dsr_submission_requirements\": [\n                {\n                    \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7004\",\n                    \"details\": \"Businesses shall design and implement methods for submitting CCPA requests and obtaining consumer consent that incorporate the following principles:\\n\\n(1) Easy to understand: The methods shall use language that is easy for consumers to read and understand. \\n\\n(2) Symmetry in choice: The path for a consumer to exercise a more privacy-protective option shall not be longer or more difficult or time-consuming than the path to exercise a less privacy-protective option because that would impair or interfere with the consumer’s ability to make a choice.\\n\\n(a) business’s process for submitting a request to opt out of sale/sharing shall not requires more steps than that business’s process for a consumer to opt-in to the sale of personal information after having previously opted out;\\n\\n(b) A choice to opt-in to the sale of personal information that only provides the two choices, “Yes” and “Ask me later,” is not equal or symmetrical;\\n\\n(c) When seeking the consumer’s consent to use their personal information, “Accept All” and “More Information,” or “Accept All” and “Preferences,” is not equal or symmetrical - because the consumer has to take additional steps to exercise their rights.\\n\\n(3) Avoid language or interactive elements that are confusing to the consumer:\\n\\n(a) The methods should not use double negatives; \\n\\n(b) Toggles or buttons must clearly indicate the consumer’s choice.\\n\\n(c) Unintuitive placement of buttons to confirm a consumer’s choice may be confusing to the consumer. \\n\\n(4) Avoid choice architecture that impairs or interferes with the consumer’s ability to make a choice. Businesses should also not design their methods in a manner that would impair the consumer’s ability to exercise their choice because consent must be freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous.\\n\\n(a) Avoid making consumers click through disruptive screens before they can exercise their rights;\\n\\n(b) Do not bundle choices so that the consumers are only offered the option to consent to allowing collection and use of personal information which is necessary for compatible purposes along with collection and use of personal information for incompatible purposes - a consumer should not be required to consent to incompatible uses of their personal information in order to obtain an expected service.  \\n\\n(5) Easy to execute. The business shall not add unnecessary burden or friction to the process by which the consumer submits a CCPA request. Methods should be tested to ensure that they are functional and do not undermine the consumer’s choice to submit the request.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"Any method may be utilized but if it does not comply with the established requirements, it may be considered a dark pattern. Any agreement obtained through the use of dark patterns shall not constitute consumer consent. \\n\\n(1) A user interface is a dark pattern if the interface has the effect of substantially subverting or impairing user autonomy, decision making, or choice;\\n\\n(2) A business’s intent in designing the interface is not determinative in whether the user interface is a dark pattern, but a factor to be considered.\\n\\n(3) If a business did not intend to design the user interface to subvert or impair user choice, but the business knows of and does not remedy a user interface that has that effect, the user interface may still be a dark pattern.\\n\\n(4) Similarly, a business’s deliberate ignorance of the effect of its user interface may also weigh in favor of establishing a dark pattern.\"\n                }\n            ],\n            \"general_exceptions_to_dsrs\": [\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"For collection, use, retention or disclosure of \\\"Deidentified\\\" consumer information, the covered entity must ensure it: \\n\\n(1) Has implemented technical safeguards that prohibit reidentification of the consumer to whom the information may pertain. \\n\\n(2) Has implemented business processes that specifically prohibit reidentification of the information. \\n\\n(3) Has implemented business processes to prevent inadvertent release of deidentified information. \\n\\n(4) Makes no attempt to reidentify the information.\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(F) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(b) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(m)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"(1) The obligations imposed on businesses by this law must not restrict a business’s ability to collect, use, retain, sell, share, or disclose consumers’ personal information that is deidentified or aggregate consumer information.\\n\\n(2) Aggregate consumer information” means information that relates to a group or category of consumers, from which individual consumer identities have been removed, that is not linked or reasonably linkable to any consumer or household, including via a device. \\n\\n(3) Deidentified means information that cannot reasonably be used to infer information about, or otherwise be linked to, a particular consumer, provided that the business that possesses the information:\\n\\n(a) takes reasonable measures to ensure that the information cannot be associated with a consumer or household;\\n\\n(b) publicly commits to maintain and use the information in deidentifled form and not to attempt to reidentify the information, except that the business may attempt to reidentify the information solely for the purpose of determining whether its deidentification processes satisfy the requirements of this law; and\\n\\n(c) contractually obligates any recipients of the information to comply with all provisions of this limitation.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"De-identified Data Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"The definitions of “medical information” and “provider of health care” in Section 56.05 of the Confidentiality of Medical Information Act shall apply and the definitions of “business associate,” “covered entity,” and “protected health information” in Section 160.103 of Title 45 of the Code of Federal Regulations shall apply.\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(c)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law shall not apply to the following: \\n\\n(1) Medical information governed by the Confidentiality of Medical Information Act (Part 2.6 (commencing with Section 56) of Division 1) or protected health information that is collected by a covered entity or business associate governed by the privacy, security, and breach notification rules issued by the United States Department of Health and Human Services, Parts 160 and 164 of Title 45 of the Code of Federal Regulations, established pursuant to the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (Public Law 104-191) and the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health Act (Public Law 111-5) \\n\\n(2) A provider of health care governed by the Confidentiality of Medical Information Act (Part 2.6 (commencing with Section 56) of Division 1) or a covered entity governed by the privacy, security, and breach notification rules issued by the United States Department of Health and Human Services, Parts 160 and 164 of Title 45 of the Code of Federal Regulations, established pursuant to the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (Public Law 104-191), to the extent the provider or covered entity maintains patient information in the same manner as medical information or protected health information as described in this law. \\n\\n(3) Personal Information collected as part of a clinical trial or other biomedical research study subject to, or conducted in accordance with, the Federal Policy for the Protection of Human Subjects, also known as the Common Rule, pursuant to good clinical practice guidelines issued by the International Council for Harmonisation or pursuant to human subject protection requirements of the United States Food and Drug Administration provided that the information is not sold or shared in a manner not permitted by this law and and if it is inconsistent, that participants be informed of that use and provide consent.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Medical Data Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"The exception shall apply only to the extent that such activity involving the collection, maintenance, disclosure, sale, communication, or use of such information by that agency, furnisher, or user is subject to regulation under the Fair Credit Reporting Act, section 1681 et seq., Title 15 of the United States Code and the information is not collected, maintained, used, communicated, disclosed, or sold except as authorized by the Fair Credit Reporting Act.\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA 1798.145(d)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law shall not apply to activity involving the collection, maintenance, disclosure, sale, communication, or use of any personal information bearing an a consumer's credit worthiness, credit standing, credit capacity, character, general reputation, personal characteristics, or made of living by a consumer reporting agency, as defined in subdivision (f) of Section 1681a of Title 15 of the United States Code, by a furnisher of information, as set forth in Section 1681s-2 of Title 15 of the United States Code, who provides information for use in a consumer report, as defined in subdivision (d) of Section 1681a of Title 15 of the United States Code, and by a user of a consumer report as set forth in Section 1681b of Title 15 of the United States Code.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Credit Data Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"This exception shall not apply to the Private Right of Action available to consumers under CPRA Section 1798.150.\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(e)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law shall not apply to personal information collected, processed, sold, or disclosed pursuant to the federal Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (Public Law 106-102), and implementing regulations, or the California Financial Information Privacy Act (Division 1.4 (commencing with Section 4050) of the Financial Code) or the Federal Farm Credit Act af 1971 (as amended in 12 U.S.C. Sections 2001 -- 2279cc and implementing regulations, 12 Code of Federal Regulations, Parts 600, et seq.).\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Financial Data Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"(1) Section 1798.120 shall not apply to vehicle information or ownership information retained or shared between a new motor vehicle dealer, as defined in Section 426 of the Vehicle Code, and the vehicle's manufacturer, as defined in Section 672 of the Vehicle Code, if the vehicle or ownership information is shared for the purpose of effectuating, or in anticipation of effectuating, a vehicle repair covered by a vehicle warranty or a recall conducted pursuant to Sections 301.18 to 30120, inclusive, of Title 49 of the United States Code, provided that the new motor vehicle dealer or vehicle manufacturer with which that vehicle information or ownership information is shared does not sell, share, or use that information for any other purpose.\\n\\n(2) For purposes of this subdivision:\\n\\n(a) \\\"Vehicle information\\\" means the vehicle information number, make, model, year, and odometer reading.\\n\\n(b) \\\"Ownership information\\\" means the name or names of the registered owner or owners and the contact information for the owner or owners.\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(f) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(g)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law shall not apply to personal information collected, processed, sold, or disclosed pursuant to the Driver’s Privacy Protection Act of 1994 (18 U.S.C. Sec. 2721 et seq.).\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Driver's Data Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(A) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(B) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(C) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(D) , US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(E)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"The obligations imposed on covered entities by this law shall not restrict its ability to:\\n\\n(1) Comply with federal, state, or local laws or comply with a court order or subpoena to provide information.\\n\\n(2) Comply with a civil, criminal, or regulatory inquiry, investigation, subpoena, or summons by federal, state, or local authorities. Law enforcement agencies, including police and sheriff's departments, may direct a business pursuant to a law enforcement agency-approved investigation with an active case number not to delete a consumer's personal information and upon receipt of such direction a business shall. not delete the personal information for 90 days, In order to allow the law enforcement agency to obtain a court-issued subpoena, order, or warrant to obtain a consumer's personal information. For good cause and only to the extent necessary for investigatory purposes, a law enforcement agency may direct a business not to delete the consumer's personal information for additional 90 day periods. A business that has received direction from a law enforcement agency not to delete the personal information of a consumer who has requested deletion of the consumer's personal information shall not use the consumer's personal information for any purpose other than retaining it to produce to law enforcement in response to a court-issued subpoena, order, or warrant, unless the consumer's deletion request is subject to an exemption from deletion under this title.\\n\\n(3) Cooperate with law enforcement agencies concerning conduct or activity that the business, service provider, or third party reasonably and in good faith believes may violate federal, state, or local law.\\n\\n(4) Cooperate with a government agency request for emergency access to a consumer's personal information if a natural person is at risk or danger of death or serious physical injury, provided that: \\n\\n(a) the request is approved by a high-ranking agency officer for emergency access to a consumer's personal information; \\n\\n(b) the request is based on the agency's good faith determination that it has a lawful basis to access the information on a non-emergency basis; and \\n\\n(c) the agency agrees to petition a court for an appropriate order within three days and to destroy the information if that order is not granted.\\n\\n(5) Exercise or defend legal claims.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Compliance with Other Federal/State Laws\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"For purposes of this law, commercial conduct takes place wholly outside of California if \\n\\n(1) the business collected that information while the consumer was outside of California, \\n\\n(2) no part of the sale of the consumer’s personal information occurred in California, and \\n\\n(3) no personal information collected while the consumer was in California is sold. \",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(1)(G)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"(1) A business may collect, sell, or share a consumer’s personal information if every aspect of that commercial conduct takes place wholly outside of California. \\n\\n(2) This shall not prohibit a business from storing, including on a device, personal information about a consumer when the consumer is in California and then collecting that personal information when the consumer and stored personal information is outside of California.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Commercial Activity Wholly Outside of California Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(b)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"Obligation to comply with DSR requests shall not apply where compliance by the business with this law would violate an evidentiary privilege under California law and shall not prevent a business from providing the personal information of a consumer to a person covered by an evidentiary privilege under California law as part of a privileged communication.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Evidentiary Privilege Exception\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(a)(2)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"(1) This law must not apply if the consumer’s personal information contains information related to accessing, procuring, or searching for services regarding contraception, pregnancy care, and perinatal care, including, but not limited to, abortion services.\\n\\n(2) This exception does not alter the use of aggregated or deidentified personal information consistent with a business purpose as defined in paragraphs (1), (2), (3), (4), (5), (7), or (8) of subdivision (e) of Section 1798.140, provided that the personal information is only retained in aggregated and deidentified form and is not sold or shared.\\n\\n(3) This exception does not alter the duty of a business to preserve or retain evidence pursuant to California or federal law in an ongoing civil proceeding.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Information related to access to perinatal care\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.145(m)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law does not apply to personal information related to employment, emergency contacts, or benefits administration. \",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Employment and Emergency Contact Information\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.146(a)(1) , US CPRA Section 1798.146(a)(2)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law does not apply to medical information and protected health information governed by:\\n\\n(1) Confidentiality of Medical Information Act (California Civil Code, Part 2.6, Section 56).\\n\\n(2) HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996) (Public Law 104-191), including its regulations.\\n\\n(3) Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health Act.\\n\\n(4) Privacy, Security, and Breach Notification Rules under Parts 160 and 164 of Title 45 of the Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), established under HIPAA.\\n\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Medical Information\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.146(a)(3)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law does not apply to business associates of a covered entity that are governed by the privacy, security, and data breach notification rules under:\\n\\n(1) Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996.\\n\\n(2) Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health Act.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Business associates of certain entities\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.146(a)(4)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law does not apply to de-identified information that meets HIPAA's deidentification standards and is derived from patient data regulated by HIPAA, the Confidentiality of Medical Information Act, or the Common Rule. If the deidentified information is reidentified, it loses the exemption and is subject to privacy and security laws like HIPAA and state regulations.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"De-identified Information \"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"applicable_circumstances\": \"\",\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.146(a)(5)\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"overview_of_exception\": \"This law does not apply to information collected, used, or disclosed in research (including clinical trials) that meets the applicable ethics, confidentiality, privacy, and security rules under:\\n\\n(1) HIPAA (Part 164 of Title 45 of the Code of Federal Regulations);\\n\\n(2) The Common Rule (Federal Policy for the Protection of Human Subjects);\\n\\n(3) Good clinical practice guidelines from the International Council for Harmonisation;\\n\\n(4) Human subject protection requirements from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration.\",\n                    \"type_of_exception\": \"Research Information\"\n                }\n            ],\n            \"general_response_form_all_dsrs\": [],\n            \"general_response_time_details\": {\n                \"circumstances_citation\": \"\",\n                \"citation\": \"\",\n                \"extension_circumstances\": \"\",\n                \"extension_obligation_citation_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                \"extension_obligation_details_to_notify_subjects\": \"\",\n                \"extension_time_period_citation\": \"\",\n                \"response_extension_time\": \"\",\n                \"response_time\": \"\",\n                \"response_time_extension_possible\": \"\",\n                \"specify_extension_time\": \"\",\n                \"specify_response_time\": \"\",\n                \"when_extension_period_commence\": \"\",\n                \"when_time_period_commence\": \"\"\n            },\n            \"language\": \"en\",\n            \"law\": {\n                \"citation\": \"US CPRA\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                \"name\": \"CPRA, 2020\",\n                \"type\": \"State\"\n            },\n            \"notification_requirements\": [\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.100(a) ,  US CPRA Regulations § 7012.\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) A covered entity that collects a protected consumer’s personal information shall, at or before the point of collection, inform protected consumers as to the categories of personal information to be collected and the purposes for which the categories of personal information shall be used. \\n\\n(2) If a business does not give the notice to the consumer at or before the point of collection of their personal information, the business shall not collect personal information from the consumer and it shall not collect additional categories of personal information or use personal information collected for additional purposes without providing the protected consumer with an updated notice.\\n\\n(3) A business shall include the following in the notice:\\n\\n(a) A list of the categories of personal information about consumers, including categories of sensitive personal information, to be collected. Each category of personal information shall be written in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of the information being collected. \\n\\n(b) The purpose(s) for which the categories of personal information, including categories of sensitive personal information, are collected and used. \\n\\n(c) Whether each category of personal information or sensitive personal information collected from the consumers is sold or shared by the business.\\n\\n(d) The length of time the business intends to retain each identified category of personal information or if that is not possible, the criteria used to determine the period of time it will be retained.\\n\\n(e) If the business sells or shares personal information, the link to the Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing  or in the case of offline notices, where the webpage can be found online. \\n\\n(f) A link to the business’s privacy policy, or in the case of offline notices, where the privacy policy can be found online. \\n\\n(4) A business neither collects nor controls the collection of personal information directly from the consumer does not need to provide notice to the consumer if it does not sell nor share the consumer’s personal information. \\n\\n(5) A data broker registered with the Attorney General pursuant to California Civil Code section 1798.99.80 et seq., where it collects personal information from a source other than directly from the consumer, does not need to provide a notice to the consumer if it has included in its registration submission a link to its online privacy policy that includes instructions on how a consumer can submit a request to opt-out of sale/sharing. \",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"The notice at collection shall be made readily available where protected consumers will encounter it at or before the point of collection of any personal information:\\n\\n(1) When a business collects consumers’ personal information online, it may post a conspicuous link to the notice on the introductory page of the business’s website and on all webpages where personal information is collected. \\n\\n(2) When a business collects consumers’ personal information through a webform, it may post a conspicuous link to the notice in close proximity to the fields in which the consumer inputs their personal information, or in close proximity to the button by which the consumer submits their personal information to the business. \\n\\n(3) When a business collects personal information through a mobile application, it may provide a link to the notice on the mobile application’s download page and within the application, such as through the application’s settings menu. \\n\\n(4) When a business collects consumers’ personal information offline, it may include the notice on printed forms that collect personal information, provide the consumer with a paper version of the notice, or post prominent signage directing consumers to where the notice can be found online. \\n\\n(5) When a business collects personal information over the telephone or in person, it may provide the notice orally. \\n\\n(6) If a business collects personal information from a consumer online:\\n\\n(i) the notice may be given to the consumer by providing a link that takes the consumer directly to the specific section of the business’s privacy policy that contains the required information;\\n\\n(ii) Directing the consumer to the beginning of the privacy policy, or to another section of the privacy policy that does not contain the required information, so that the consumer is required to scroll through other information in order to\\ndetermine the categories of personal information to be collected and/or whether the business sells or shares the personal information collected, does not satisfy this standard.\\n\\n(7) first party and third parties may provide a single Notice at Collection that includes the required information about their collective Information Practices.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.120(b) , US CPRA Section 1798.135 , US CPRA Regulations § 7013\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) A covered entity that sells consumers’ personal information to third parties shall provide notice to protected consumers, containing a clear and conspicuous right titled “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” along with a description of the right and the means to exercise it, which shall enable a consumer, or a person authorized by the consumer, to opt out of the sale or sharing of the consumer’s personal information by the covered entity. \\n\\n(2) The purpose of the Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing is to inform consumers of their right to direct a business that sells or shares their personal information to stop selling or sharing their personal information and to provide them with the opportunity to exercise that right.\\n\\n(3) A business shall include the following in its Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing: \\n\\n(a) A description of the consumer’s right to opt-out of the sale or sharing of their personal information by the business; and\\n\\n(b) Instructions on how the consumer can submit a request to opt-out of sale/sharing: \\n\\n(i) If notice is provided online, the notice shall include the interactive form by which the consumer can submit their request to opt-out of sale/sharing online;\\n\\n(ii) If the business does not operate a website, the notice shall explain the offline method by which the consumer can submit their request to opt-out of sale/sharing.\\n\\n(4) A business does not need to provide a Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing or the “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” link if: \\n\\n(a) It does not sell or share personal information; and\\n\\n(b) It states in its privacy policy that it does not sell or share personal information. \\n\\n(5) A business shall not sell or share the personal information it collected during the time it did not have this notice posted, unless it obtains the consent of the consumer. \",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"A business shall also provide the notice to opt-out of sale/sharing in the same manner in which it collects the personal information that it sells or shares. \\n\\n(1) A business shall post the Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing on the internet webpage to which the consumer is directed after clicking on the “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” link or If clicking on the “Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information” link immediately effectuates the consumer’s right to opt-out of sale/sharing or if the business processes opt-out preference signals in a frictionless manner and chooses not to post a link, the business shall provide the notice within its privacy policy.\\n\\n(2) A business that sells or shares personal information that it collects in the course of interacting with consumers offline, such as in a brick-and-mortar store, shall provide notice through an offline method.\\n\\n(3) A business that sells or shares personal information that it collects over the phone shall provide notice orally during the call when the information is collected. \"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.121 , US CPRA Section 1798.135 , US CPRA Regulations § 7014\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) A covered entity that collects a protected consumer’s personal information shall provide the consumer with the right, at any time, to direct the covered entity that collects sensitive personal information about the consumer to limit its use of the consumer's sensitive personal information to that use which is necessary to perform the services or provide the goods reasonably expected by an average consumer who requests such goods or services, or to perform the services set forth in paragraphs (2), (4), (5), and (8) of subdivision (e) of US CPRA Section 1798.140 , and as authorized by regulations adopted pursuant to subparagraph (C) of paragraph (19) of subdivision (a) of US CPRA Section 1798.185. A covered entity that uses or discloses a consumer's sensitive personal information for purposes other than those specified in this subdivision shall provide notice to consumers, pursuant to subdivision (a) of US CPRA Section 1798.135, that this information may be used, or disclosed to a service provider or contractor, for additional, specified purposes and that consumers have the right to limit the use or disclosure of their sensitive personal information.\\n\\n(2) The purpose of the Notice of Right to Limit is to inform consumers of their right to limit a business’s use and disclosure of their sensitive personal information and to provide them with the opportunity to exercise that right. \\n\\n(3) A business shall include the following in its Notice of Right to Limit:\\n\\n(a) A description of the consumer’s right to limit; and\\n\\n(b) Instruction on how the consumer can submit a request to limit: \\n\\n(i) If notice is provided online, the notice shall include the interactive form by which the consumer can submit their request to limit online;\\n\\n(ii) If the business does not operate a website, the notice shall explain the offline method by which the consumer can submit their request to limit. \\n\\n(4) A business does not need to provide a Notice of Right to Limit or the “Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information” link if:\\n\\n(a) It only uses and discloses sensitive personal information that it collected about the consumer for exempted purposes and and states so in its privacy policy; or\\n\\n(b) It only collects or processes sensitive personal information without the purpose of inferring characteristics about a consumer, and states so in its privacy policy.\\n\\n(5) A business shall not use or disclose for any unexempted purposes sensitive personal information it collected during the time the business did not have the notice posted unless it obtains the consent of the consumer. \",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"A business that uses or discloses a consumer’s sensitive personal information for purposes beyond those specified as necessary to perform the services or provide the goods reasonably expected by an average consumer who requests such goods or services, or to perform the services set forth in paragraphs (2), (4), (5), and (8) of subdivision (e) of US CPRA Section 1798.140 , and as authorized by regulations adopted pursuant to subparagraph (C) of paragraph (19) of subdivision (a) of US CPRA Section 1798.185 shall provide the Notice of Right to Limit to consumers as follows: \\n\\n(1) A business shall post the notice on the internet webpage to which the consumer is directed after clicking on the “Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information” link. If clicking on the “Limit the Use of My Sensitive Personal Information” link immediately effectuates the consumer’s right to limit, the business shall provide the notice within its privacy policy. \\n\\n(2) A business that does not operate a website shall establish, document, and comply with another method by which it informs consumers of their right to limit. \"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.125(b)(2) , US CPRA Section 1798.135 , US CPRA Regulations § 7019\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) The purpose of the Notice of Financial Incentive is to explain to the consumer the material terms of a financial incentive or price or service difference the business is offering so that the consumer may make an informed decision about whether to participate. \\n\\n(2) A business that does not offer a financial incentive or price or service difference is not required to provide this notice.\\n\\n(3) A business shall include the following in its Notice of Financial Incentive:\\n\\n(a) A succinct summary of the financial incentive or price or service difference offered;\\n\\n(b) A description of the material terms of the financial incentive or price or service difference, including the categories of personal information that are implicated by the financial incentive or price or service difference and the value of the consumer’s data;\\n\\n(c) How the consumer can opt-in to the financial incentive or price or service difference;\\n\\n(d) A statement of the consumer’s right to withdraw from the financial incentive at any time and how the consumer may exercise that right; and\\n\\n(e) An explanation of how the price or service difference is reasonably related to the value of the consumer’s data, including: \\n\\n(i) A good-faith estimate of the value of the consumer’s data that forms the basis for offering the price or service difference; and\\n\\n(ii) A description of the method(s) the business used to calculate the value of the consumer’s data.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"The Notice of Financial Incentive shall be readily available where consumers will encounter it before opting-in to the financial incentive or price or service difference. If the business offers the financial incentive or price or service difference online, the notice may be given by providing a link that takes the consumer directly to the specific section of a business’s privacy policy that contains the information.\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.130(a)(5) , US CPRA Regulations § 7011\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1)  business shall, in a form that is reasonably accessible to consumers, disclose information about its online and offline Information Practices in its online privacy policy or policies if the business has an online privacy policy or policies and in any California-specific description of consumers’ privacy rights, or if the business does not maintain those policies, on its internet website and update that information at least once every 12 months.\\n\\n(2) The purpose of the privacy policy is to provide consumers with a comprehensive description of a business’s online and offline Information Practices It shall also inform consumers about the rights they have regarding their personal information and provide any information necessary for them to exercise those rights. \\n\\n(3) The privacy policy shall include the following information: \\n\\n(a) A comprehensive description of the business’s online and offline Information Practices which includes the following: \\n\\n(i) Identification of the categories of personal information the business has collected about consumers in the preceding 12 months. The categories shall be described using the specific terms set forth in US CPRA Section 1798.140 (v)(1)(A) - (K) and US CPRA Section 1798.140 (ae)(1) - (3). To the extent that the business has discretion in its description, the business shall describe the category in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of the information being collected.\\n\\n(ii) Identification of the categories of sources from which the personal information is collected. \\n\\n(iii) Identification of the specific business or commercial purpose for collecting personal information from consumers. The purpose shall be described in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of why the information is collected.\\n\\n(iv) Identification of the categories of personal information, if any, that the business has sold or shared to third parties in the preceding 12 months. If the business has not sold or shared consumers’ personal information in the preceding 12 months, the business shall disclose that fact.\\n\\n(vi) For each category of identified personal information , the categories of third parties to whom the information was sold or shared.\\n\\n(vii) Identification of the specific business or commercial purpose for selling or sharing consumers’ personal information. The purpose shall be described in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of why the information is sold or shared. \\n\\n(viii) A statement regarding whether the business has actual knowledge that it sells or shares the personal information of consumers under 16 years of age. \\n\\n(ix) Identification of the categories of personal information, if any, that the business has disclosed for a business purpose to third parties in the preceding 12 months. If the business has not disclosed consumers’ personal information for a business purpose in the preceding 12 months, the business shall disclose that fact.\\n\\n(x) For each category of identified personal information, the categories of third parties to whom the information was disclosed. \\n\\n(xi) Identification of the specific business or commercial purpose for disclosing the consumer’s personal information. The purpose shall be described in a manner that provides consumers a meaningful understanding of why the information is disclosed.\\n\\n(xii) A statement regarding whether he business uses or discloses sensitive personal information for purposes other than exempt purposes.\\n\\n(b) An explanation of the rights that this law confers on consumers regarding their personal information, which includes the following: \\n\\n(i) The right to know what personal information the business has collected about the consumer, including the categories of personal information, the categories of sources from which the personal information is collected, the business or commercial purpose for collecting, selling, or sharing personal information, the categories of third parties to whom the business discloses personal information, and the specific pieces of personal information the business has collected about the consumer;\\n\\n(ii) The right to delete personal information that the business has collected from the consumer, subject to certain exceptions;\\n\\n(iii) The right to correct inaccurate personal information that a business maintains about a consumer;\\n\\n(iv) If the business sells or shares personal information, the right to opt-out of the sale or sharing of their personal information by the business;\\n\\n(v) If the business uses or discloses sensitive personal information for reasons other than exempt purposes, the right to limit the use or disclosure of sensitive personal information by the business.\\n\\n(vi) The right not to receive discriminatory treatment by the business for the exercise of privacy rights conferred by the law, including an employee’s, applicant’s, or independent contractor’s right not to be retaliated against for the exercise of their rights under the law.\\n\\n(c) An explanation of how consumers can exercise their CCPA rights and what consumers can expect from that process, which includes the following:  \\n\\n(i) An explanation of the methods by which the consumer can exercise their rights under this law;\\n\\n(ii) Instructions for submitting a request under the this law, including any links to an online request form or portal for making such a request, if offered by the business; \\n\\n(iii) If the business sells or shares personal information, and is required to provide a Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing, the contents of the Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing or a link to that notice.\\n\\n(iv) If the business uses or discloses sensitive personal information for purposes other than exempt purposes, and is required to provide a Notice of Right to Limit, the contents of the Notice of Right to Limit or a link to that notice.\\n\\n(v) A general description of the process the business uses to verify a consumer request to know, request to delete, and request to correct, when applicable, including any information the consumer must provide; \\n\\n(vi) Explanation of how an opt-out preference signal will be processed for the consumer (i.e., whether the signal applies to the device, browser, consumer account, and/or offline sales, and in what circumstances) and how the consumer can use an opt-out preference signal;\\n\\n(vii) If the business processes opt-out preference signals in a frictionless manner, information on how consumers can implement opt-out preference signals for the business to process in a frictionless manner; \\n\\n(viii) Instructions on how an authorized agent can make a request under the CCPA on the consumer’s behalf;\\n\\n(ix) If the business has actual knowledge that it sells the personal information of consumers under 16 years of age, a description of the processes required to take consent of the consumer or their parents.\\n\\n(x) A contact for questions or concerns about the business’s privacy policies and Information Practices using a method reflecting the manner in which the business primarily interacts with the consumer. \\n\\n(d) Date the privacy policy was last updated. \\n\\n(e) If subject to the data reporting requirements under CPRA Regulations 7102 - the information or link to the information required to be published.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"(1) The privacy policy shall be available in a format that allows a consumer to print it out as a document. \\n\\n(2) The privacy policy shall be posted online and accessible through a conspicuous link using the word “privacy” on the business’s website Homepage(s) or on the download or landing page of a mobile application.\\n\\n(3) If the business has a California-specific description of consumers’ privacy rights on its website, then the privacy policy shall be included in that description. \\n\\n(4) A business that does not operate a website shall make the privacy policy conspicuously available to consumers.\\n\\n(5) A mobile application may include a link to the privacy policy in the application’s settings menu. \"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.115(d)\",\n                    \"details\": \"A third party which has gained personal information of a protected consumer must notify the consumer before it further sells or shares the personal information with another third party and it must provide an opportunity to the consumer to opt-out of the sale of their personal information before selling or sharing the personal information.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"methods\": \"\"\n                }\n            ],\n            \"obligation_inform_subject_about_rights\": [\n                {\n                    \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.135(c)(2) , US CPRA Section 1798.135(c)(3)\",\n                    \"details\": \"A covered entity must provide a description of a consumer’s rights and one or more designated methods for submitting requests and all employees tasked with consumers' queries should be trained with knowledge on consumer's rights and how to direct consumers to exercise their rights under those sections.\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"methods\": \"(1) Privacy Policy \\n\\n(2) Employees dealing with consumers\"\n                }\n            ],\n            \"penalties\": [\n                {\n                    \"action_enforcer\": \"CPPA\",\n                    \"aggravating_mitigating_factors\": \"The agency and any court, as applicable, shall consider the good faith cooperation of the business, service provider, contractor, or other person in determining the amount of any administrative fine or civil penalty for a violation of this law. \",\n                    \"amount_awarded\": {\n                        \"max\": \"$7988\",\n                        \"min\": \"$2663\"\n                    },\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.155(b) , US CPRA Section 1798.199.45 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.50 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.55 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.70 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.85 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.100 , US CPRA Section 1798.185(5)(a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7300 , US CPRA Regulations § 7301 , US CPRA Regulations § 7302 \",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) Any covered entity that violates this law shall be subject to an injunction and liable for a civil penalty of not more than two thousand six hundred and sixty-six dollars ($2,663 ) for each violation or seven thousand nine hundred and eighty eight dollars ($7,988 ) for each intentional violation or violations involving the personal information of consumers whom the business, service provider, contractor or other person has actual knowledge is under 16 years of age which shall be assessed and recovered in a civil action.\\n\\n(2) The Agency may initiate investigations from referrals from government agencies or private organizations, and sworn, nonsworn, or anonymous complaints, or on the Agency’s own initiative. \\n\\n(3) Upon receiving the sworn complaint of any person or on its own initiative:\\n\\n(a) the CPPA may investigate possible violations of this law relating to any business, service provider, contractor, or person. The agency may decide not to investigate a complaint. In making a decision not to investigate the CPPA may consider:\\n\\n(i) Lack of intent to violate this law.\\n\\n(ii) Voluntary efforts undertaken by the business, service provider, contractor, or person to cure the alleged violation prior to being notified by the agency of the complaint.\\n\\n(iii) As part of the CPPAs decision to pursue investigations of possible or alleged violations of this law, the Agency may consider all facts it determines to be relevant, including the amount of time between the effective date of the statutory or regulatory requirement(s) and the possible or alleged violation(s) of those requirements, and good faith efforts to comply with those requirements\\n\\n(b) The CPPA shall notify in writing the person who made the complaint of the action, if any, the agency has taken or plans to take on the complaint, together with the reasons for that action or nonaction.\\n\\n(4) A complaint must:\\n\\n(a) Identify the business, service provider, contractor, or person who allegedly violated the law;\\n\\n(b) State the facts that support each alleged violation and include any documents or other evidence supporting this conclusion;\\n\\n(c) Authorize the alleged violator and Agency to communicate regarding the complaint, including disclosing the complaint and any information relating to the complaint;\\n\\n(d) Include the name and current contact information of the complainant; and\\n\\n(e) Be signed and submitted under penalty of perjury. \\n\\n(5) After the investigation The Enforcement Division will provide the alleged violator with notice of the probable cause proceeding\\n\\n(a) The proceeding shall be closed to the public unless the alleged violator files, at least 10 business days before the proceeding, a written request for a public proceeding. If the proceeding is not open to the public, then the proceeding may be conducted in whole or in part by telephone or videoconference. \\n\\n(b) The Agency shall conduct the proceeding informally. \\n\\n(i) Only the alleged violator(s), their legal counsel, and Enforcement Division shall have the right to participate at the proceeding. \\n\\n(ii) The Agency shall determine whether there is probable cause based on the probable cause notice and any information or arguments presented at the probable cause proceeding by the parties. \\n\\n(iv) If the alleged violator(s) fails to participate or appear at the probable cause proceeding, the alleged violator(s) waives the right to further probable cause proceedings and the Agency shall determine whether there is probable cause based on the notice and any information or argument provided by the Enforcement Division. \\n\\n(c) The Agency shall issue a written decision with its probable cause determination and serve it on the alleged violator electronically or by mail. The Agency’s probable cause determination is final and not subject to appeal. \\n\\n(6) When the agency determines there is probable cause for believing this law has been violated, it shall hold a hearing to determine if a violation has or violations have occurred. Notice shall be given and the hearing conducted in accordance with the Administrative Procedure Act (Chapter 5 (commencing with Section 11500), Part 1, Division 3, Title 2, Government Code).\\n\\n(a) If the agency determines on the basis of the hearing conducted that a violation or violations have occurred, it shall issue an order that may require the violator to do all or any of the following:\\n\\n(i) Cease and desist violation of this title.\\n\\n(ii) Issue an administrative fine \\n\\n(b) When the agency determines that no violation has occurred, it shall publish a declaration saying so.\\n\\n(7) If two or more persons are responsible for any violation or violations, they shall be jointly and severally liable.\\n\\n(8) No administrative action brought pursuant to this title alleging a violation of any of the provisions of this title shall be commenced more than five years after the date on which the violation occurred.\\n\\n(a) service of the probable cause hearing notice, constitutes the commencement of the administrative action.\\n\\n(b) If the person alleged to have violated this law engages in the fraudulent concealment of the person’s acts or identity, the five-year period shall be tolled for the period of the concealment.\\n\\n(c) If, upon being ordered by a superior court to produce any documents sought by a subpoena in any administrative proceeding under this law, the person alleged to have violated this law fails to produce documents in response to the order by the date ordered to comply therewith, the five-year period shall be tolled for the period of the delay from the date of filing of the motion to compel until the date the documents are produced.\\n\\n(9) (a) If the time for judicial review of a final agency order or decision has lapsed, or if all means of judicial review of the order or decision have been exhausted, the agency may apply to the clerk of the court for a judgment to collect the administrative fines imposed by the order or decision, or the order as modified in accordance with a decision on judicial review.\\n\\n(a) The agency may bring an application only within four years after the date on which all means of judicial review of the order or decision have been exhausted.\\n\\n(10) Any decision of the CPPA with respect to a complaint or administrative fine shall be subject to judicial review in an action brought by an interested party to the complaint or administrative fine and shall be subject to an abuse of discretion standard.\\n\\n(11) the monetary thresholds for Administrative Fines for violation of this law will be readjusted by the CPPA in January of every odd-numbered year to reflect any increase in the Consumer Price Index.\",\n                    \"exceptions\": \"\",\n                    \"exceptions_citations\": \"\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"punishment_type\": \"Administrative\",\n                    \"type\": \"Regulatory Penalty\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"action_enforcer\": \"CPPA\",\n                    \"aggravating_mitigating_factors\": \"\",\n                    \"amount_awarded\": {\n                        \"max\": \"\",\n                        \"min\": \"\"\n                    },\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.199.75\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) In addition to other available remedies, the CPPA may bring a civil action and obtain a judgment in superior court for the purpose of collecting any unpaid administrative fines imposed pursuant to this law after exhaustion of judicial review of the agency’s action. \\n\\n(a) The action may be filed as a small claims, limited civil, or unlimited civil case depending on the jurisdictional amount. The venue for this action shall be in the county where the administrative fines were imposed by the agency.\\n\\n(b) A civil action shall be commenced within four years after the date on which the administrative fines were imposed.\",\n                    \"exceptions\": \"\",\n                    \"exceptions_citations\": \"\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"punishment_type\": \"Civil\",\n                    \"type\": \"Civil Penalty\"\n                },\n                {\n                    \"action_enforcer\": \"Attorney General\",\n                    \"aggravating_mitigating_factors\": \"(1) The court may consider the good faith cooperation of the business, service provider, contractor, or other person in determining the amount of the civil penalty.\\n\\n(2) The agency and any court, as applicable, shall consider the good faith cooperation of the business, service provider, contractor, or other person in determining the amount of any administrative fine or civil penalty for a violation of this law. \",\n                    \"amount_awarded\": {\n                        \"max\": \"$7988\",\n                        \"min\": \"$2663\"\n                    },\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.199.90 , US CPRA Section 1798.185(5)(a) \",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) Any business, service provider, contractor, or other person that violates this law shall be subject to an injunction and liable for a civil penalty of not more than two thousand six hundred and sixty-three dollars ($2,663) for each violation or seven thousand nine hundred and eighty eight dollars ($7,988) for each intentional violation and each violation involving the personal information of minor consumers which shall be assessed and recovered in a civil action brought in the name of the people of the State of California by the Attorney General.\\n\\n(2) The agency shall, upon request by the Attorney General, stay an administrative action or investigation under this law to permit the Attorney General to proceed with an investigation or civil action and shall not pursue an administrative action or investigation, unless the Attorney General subsequently determines not to pursue an investigation or civil action. The agency may not limit the authority of the Attorney General to enforce this law.\\n\\n(3) No civil action may be filed by the Attorney General for any violation of this law after the CPPA has issued a decision against that person for the same violation.\\n\\n(4) The monetary thresholds for Civil Penalties for violation of this law will be readjusted by the CPPA in January of every odd-numbered year to reflect any increase in the Consumer Price Index.\",\n                    \"exceptions\": \"\",\n                    \"exceptions_citations\": \"\",\n                    \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                    \"punishment_type\": \"Civil\",\n                    \"type\": \"Civil Penalty\"\n                }\n            ],\n            \"personal_info\": {\n                \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.140(v) , US CPRA Section 1798.140(ae)\",\n                \"clarifications\": \"(1) Personal Information does not include consumer information that is deidentified or aggregate consumer information.\\n\\n(2) Personal information does not include publicly available information or lawfully obtained, truthful information that is a matter of public concern. \\n\\n(a) Publicly Available information means information that is:\\n\\n(i) lawfully made available from federal, state, or local government records, or \\n\\n(ii) information that a business has a reasonable basis to believe is lawfully made available to the general public by the consumer or from widely distributed media; or \\n\\n(iii) information made available by a person to whom the consumer has disclosed the information if the consumer has not restricted the information to a specific audience.\\n\\n(c) Publicly available information does not mean biometric information collected by a business about a consumer without the consumer's knowledge.\",\n                \"definition\": \"(1) Personal information means information that identifies, relates to, describes, is reasonably capable of being associated with, or could reasonably be linked, directly or indirectly, with a particular consumer or household. Personal information includes, but is not limited to, the following:\\n\\n(a) Identifiers such as a real name, alias, postal address, unique personal identifier, online identifier, internet protocol address, email address, account name, social security number, driver’s license number, passport number, or other similar identifiers.\\n\\n(b) Any  personal information described in subdivision (e) of Section 1798.80 of the California Civil Code - any information that identifies, relates to, describes, or is capable of being associated with, a particular individual, including, but not limited to, his or her name, signature, social security number, physical characteristics or description, address, telephone number, passport number, driver’s license or state identification card number, insurance policy number, education, employment, employment history, bank account number, credit card number, debit card number, or any other financial information, medical information, or health insurance information. “Personal information” does not include publicly available information that is lawfully made available to the general public from federal, state, or local government records. \\n\\n(c) Characteristics of protected classifications under California or federal law.\\n\\n(d) Commercial information, including records of personal property, products or services purchased, obtained, or considered, or other purchasing or consuming histories or tendencies.\\n\\n(e) Biometric information.\\n\\n(f) Internet or other electronic network activity information, including, but not limited to, browsing history, search history, and information regarding a consumer’s interaction with an Internet Web siteinternet website, application, or advertisement.\\n\\n(g) Geolocation data.\\n\\n(h) Audio, electronic, visual, thermal, olfactory, or similar information.\\n\\n(i) Professional or employment-related information.\\n\\n(j) Education information, defined as information that is not publicly available personally identifiable information as defined in the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (20 U.S.C. Sec. 1232g; 34 C.F.R. Part 99).\\n\\n(k) Inferences drawn from any of the information identified in this subdivision to create a profile about a consumer reflecting the consumer’s preferences, characteristics, psychological trends, predispositions, behavior, attitudes, intelligence, abilities, and aptitudes.\\n\\n(2) Sensitive personal information means: \\n\\n(a) personal information that reveals:\\n\\n(i) a consumer's social security, driver's license, state identification card, or passport number; \\n\\n(ii) a consumer's account log-In, financial account, debit card, or credit card number in combination with any required security or access code, password, or credentials allowing access to an account; \\n\\n(iii) a consumer's precise geolocation; \\n\\n(iv) a consumer's racial or ethnic origin, citizenship or immigration status, religious or philosophical beliefs, or union membership; \\n\\n(v) the contents of a consumer's mall, email and text messages, unless the business is the intended recipient of the communication; \\n\\n(vi) a consumer's genetic data; and \\n\\n(b) the processing of: \\n\\n(i) biometric information for the purpose of uniquely identifying a consumer; \\n\\n(ii) personal information collected and analyzed concerning a consumer's health; or \\n\\n(iii) personal information collected and analyzed concerning a consumer's sex life or sexual orientation.\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                \"types_of_pi_covered\": \"(1) Personal Information\\n\\n(2) Sensitive Personal Information\"\n            },\n            \"protected_consumers\": {\n                \"citation\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.140(i)\",\n                \"definition\": \"A natural person who is a California resident, as defined in Section 17014 of Title 18 of the California Code of Regulations, as that section read on September 1, 2017, however identified, including by any unique identifier\",\n                \"details\": \"Any permanent and registered resident of California is covered.\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\"\n            },\n            \"regulatory_body\": {\n                \"address\": \"2101 Arena Blvd Sacramento, CA 95834\",\n                \"citations\": \" US CPRA Section 1798.199.10 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.40 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.55 , US CPRA Section 1798.199.65 , US CPRA Section 1798.185\",\n                \"details\": \"CPRA established the California Privacy Protection Agency (CPPA) as a regulatory body with full administrative power and jurisdiction to interpret the provisions of the CPRA and enforce the prescribed sanctions and penalties in case of any violations. The sole purpose of the agency is to protect the privacy rights of Californians. As per the CPRA, the CPPA shall be governed by a five-member board, including a chairperson. \",\n                \"email\": \"info@cppa.ca.gov\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4.\",\n                \"name\": \"California Privacy Protection Agency (CPPA)\",\n                \"phone_number\": \"(916) 445-9555\",\n                \"powers\": \"The CPPA shall perform the following functions:\\n\\n(1) Administer, implement, and enforce this law through administrative actions.\\n\\n(a) The CPPA shall have all the powers granted by Administrative Procedure Act (Chapter 5 (commencing with Section 11500), Part 1, Division 3, Title 2, Government Code to conduct administrative hearings.\\n\\n(b) The CPPA may subpoena witnesses, compel their attendance and testimony, administer oaths and affirmations, take evidence and require by subpoena the production of any books, papers, records, or other items material to the performance of the agency’s duties or exercise of its powers, including, but not limited to, its power to audit a business’ compliance with this law.\\n\\n(2) On and after the earlier of July l , 2021, or within 6 months of the Agency providing the Attorney General with notice that it is prepared to assume rulemaking responsibilities under this law and shall adopt, amend, and rescind the regulations pursuant to CPRA Section 1798.185 to carry out the purposes and provisions of this law, including regulations specifying record keeping requirements for businesses to ensure compliance with this law.\\n\\n(3) Through the implementation of this law, protect the fundamental privacy rights of natural persons with respect to the use of their personal information.\\n\\n(4) Promote public awareness and understanding of the risks, rules, responsibilities, safeguards, and rights in relation to the collection, use, sale and disclosure of personal information, including the rights of minors with respect to their own information, and provide a public report summarizing the risk assessments filed with the Agency while ensuring that data security is not compromised.\\n\\n(5) Provide guidance to consumers regarding their rights under this law.\\n\\n(6) Provide guidance to businesses regarding their duties and responsibilities under this law, and appoint a Chief Privacy Auditor to conduct audits of businesses to ensure compliance:\\n\\n(a) The CPPA may conduct an audit to investigate possible violations of this law. \\n\\n(b) Alternatively, the Agency may conduct an audit if the subject’s collection or processing of personal information presents significant risk to consumer privacy or security, or if the subject has a history of noncompliance with the law or any other privacy protection law. \\n\\n(7) Provide technical assistance and advice to the California Legislature, upon request, with respect to privacy-related legislation.\\n\\n(8) Monitor relevant developments relating to the protection of personal information, and in particular, the development of information and communication technologies and commercial practices.\\n\\n(9) Cooperate with other agencies with jurisdiction over privacy laws and with data processing authorities in California, other states, territories, and countries to ensure consistent application of privacy protections.\\n\\n(10) Establish a mechanism pursuant to which persons doing business in California that do not meet the definition of business under this law may voluntarily certify that they are in compliance with the requirements and make a list of such entities available to the public.\\n\\n(11) Solicit, review, and approve applications for grants to the extent funds are available \\n\\n(12) Perform all other acts necessary or appropriate in the exercise of its power, authority, and jurisdiction, and seek to balance the goals of strengthening consumer privacy while giving attention to the impact on businesses.\\n\\n(13) Adjust the monetary thresholds for Administrative and Civil Penalties for violation of this law in January of every odd-numbered year to reflect any increase in the Consumer Price Index.\",\n                \"website\": \"https://cppa.ca.gov/about_us/contact.html\"\n            },\n            \"request_refusal\": {\n                \"any_prescribed_format_link\": \"\",\n                \"citations\": \"US CPRA 1798.145(i)(2) , US CPRA 1798.145(i)(3) , US CPRA Regulations § 7021(b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7022 (g) , US CPRA Regulations § 7023 (a) , US CPRA Regulations § 7023 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7023 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7023 (g) , US CPRA Regulations § 7023 (h) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (b) , US CPRA Regulations § 7024 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (e) , US CPRA Regulations § 7026 (j) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (f) , US CPRA Regulations § 7027 (j) , US CPRA Regulations § 7062 (f)\",\n                \"details\": \"(1) Covered entities may refuse to take action on the DSR requests of a consumer if they are found to be manifestly unfounded or excessive, in particular because of their repetitive character.\\n\\n(2) Covered entities may refuse to take action on the DSR requests of a consumer if they cannot verify the consumer within the 45-day time period of receiving a request to delete, request to correct, and request to know, the business may deny the request. \\n\\n(3) A covered entity may deny a consumer’s request to correct if \\n\\n(a) it determines that the contested personal information is more likely than not accurate based on the totality of the circumstances. \\n\\n(b) it has denied the consumer’s request to correct the same alleged inaccuracy within the past six months of receiving the request. However, the business must treat the request to correct as new if the consumer provides new or additional documentation to prove that the information at issue is inaccurate.\\n\\n(c) it has a good-faith, reasonable, and documented belief that a request to correct is fraudulent or abusive. The business shall inform the requestor that it will not comply with the request and shall provide an explanation why it believes the request is fraudulent or abusive. \\n\\n(4) A covered entity may \\n\\n(a) deny a request to Opt-out of Sale or Sharing of Personal Information:\\n\\n(i) If a business has a good-faith, reasonable, and documented belief that the request is fraudulent;\\n\\n(ii) if the agent who made the request on the consumer's behalf does not provide to the business the consumer’s signed permission demonstrating that they have been authorized by the consumer to act on his/her behalf - The requirement to obtain and provide written permission from the consumer does not apply to requests made by an opt-out preference signal.\\n\\n(b) deny a request to Limit the Use of Sensitive Personal Information: \\n\\n(i) If it has a good-faith, reasonable, and documented belief that a request to Limit the Use of Sensitive Personal Information is fraudulent;\\n\\n(ii) if the agent who made the request on the consumer's behalf does not provide to the business the consumer’s signed permission demonstrating that they have been authorized by the consumer to act on his/her behalf - The requirement to obtain and provide written permission from the consumer does not apply to requests made by an opt-out preference signal.\\n\\n(5) In cases where a business denies a consumer’s request to delete in whole or in part, the business shall do all of the following: \\n\\n(a) Delete the consumer’s personal information that is not subject to the exception; \\n\\n(b) Not use the consumer’s personal information retained for any other purpose than provided for by that exception; and\\n\\n(c) Instruct its service providers and contractors to delete the consumer’s personal information that is not subject to the exception and to not use the consumer’s personal information retained for any purpose other than the purpose provided for by that exception.\\n\\n(6) If a business denies a consumer’s verified request to know specific pieces of personal information, in part, because of a conflict with federal or state law, or an exception within the law, the business the business shall disclose the other information sought by the consumer. \\n\\n(a) For requests that seek the disclosure of specific pieces of information about the consumer, if a business cannot verify the identity of the person making the request,  business shall not disclose any specific pieces of personal information to the requestor. If the request is denied in whole or in part, the business shall also evaluate the consumer’s request as if it is seeking the disclosure of categories of personal information.\\n\\n(b) For requests that seek the disclosure of categories of personal information about the consumer, if a business cannot verify the identity of the person making the request he business may deny the request to disclose the categories and other information requested.\\n\\n(c) For requests that seek personal information the business has collected and maintained about a consumer on or after January 1, 2022, including beyond the 12-month period preceding the business’s receipt of the request, the business can reject the request if it proves impossible or would involve disproportionate effort or the consumer requests data for a specific time period.\",\n                \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                \"refusal_communication_to_subject\": \"(1) Covered entities must communicate the reasons for refusal to act on the DSR request along with any rights the consumer may have to appeal the decision (to the covered entity) without delay and within 45 days at the most to the consumer.\\n\\n(2) Covered entities must notify the consumer of the reasons for refusing the request if they are choosing to refuse action on the basis of the DSR requests from the consumer being manifestly unfounded or excessive.\\n\\n(3) If a covered entity rejects a request due to a failure of verification, it shall inform the requestors that their identity cannot be verified. \\n\\n(4) If a covered entity rejects a request due to a good faith reasonable belief that it is fraudulent, it shall inform the requestors that it will not comply with the request and shall provide an explanation why it believes the request is fraudulent or abusive. \\n\\n(5) If the business denies a consumer’s request to delete in whole or in part, the business shall \\n\\n(a) provide to the consumer a detailed explanation of the basis for the denial, including any conflict with federal or state law, the exception allowed within the law or factual basis for contending that compliance would be impossible or involve disproportionate effort, unless prohibited from doing so by law.\\n\\n(b) provide the consumer the link to or the contents of the Notice of the Right to Opt-out of Sale and Sharing of Personal Information if it sells or shares personal information and the consumer has not already made a request to opt-out of sale/sharing.\\n\\n(6) If the business denies a consumer’s request to correct in whole or in part: \\n\\n(a) the business shall explain the basis for the denial, including any conflict with federal or state law, the exception allowed within the law, the inadequacy in the required documentation, or contention that compliance proves impossible or involves disproportionate effort. \\n\\n(b) If a business claims that complying with the consumer’s request to correct would be impossible or would involve disproportionate effort, the business shall provide the consumer a detailed explanation that includes enough facts to give a consumer a\\nmeaningful understanding as to why the business cannot comply with the request. The business shall not simply state that it is impossible or would require disproportionate effort. \\n\\n(c) If a business denies a consumer’s request to correct personal information collected and analyzed concerning a consumer’s health, the business shall also inform the consumer that they may provide a written statement to the business to be made part of\\nthe consumer’s record. The business shall explain to the consumer that the written statement is limited to 250 words per alleged inaccurate piece of personal information and shall include that the consumer must request that the statement be made part of the consumer’s record. Upon receipt of such a statement, the business shall include it with the consumer’s record.\\n\\n(d) If the personal information at issue can be deleted pursuant to a request to delete, inform the consumer that they can make a request to delete the personal information and provide instructions on how the consumer can make a request to delete. \\n\\n(7) If a business denies a consumer’s verified request to know specific pieces of personal information, in whole or in part, because of a conflict with federal or state law, or an exception within the law, the business shall inform the requestor and explain the basis for the denial, unless prohibited from doing so by law. \\n\\n(a) For requests that seek the disclosure of specific pieces of information about the consumer, if a business cannot verify the identity of the person making the request, the business shall not disclose any specific pieces of personal information to the requestor and shall inform the requestor that it cannot verify their identity. \\n\\n(b) For requests that seek the disclosure of categories of personal information about the consumer, if a business cannot verify the identity of the person making the request he business may deny the request to disclose the categories and other information requested and shall inform the requestor that it cannot verify their identity - If the request is denied in whole or in part, the business shall provide or direct the consumer to its Information Practices  set forth in its privacy policy. \\n\\n(c) In rejecting an access request personal information it has collected and maintained about a consumer on or after January 1, 2022, including beyond the 12-month period preceding the business’s receipt of the request because it proves impossible or would involve disproportionate effort or the consumer requests data for a specific time period, the business shall provide the consumer a detailed explanation that includes enough facts to give a consumer a meaningful understanding as to why the business cannot provide personal information beyond the 12-month period. The business shall not simply state that it is impossible or would require disproportionate effort.\"\n            },\n            \"rights_of_children\": {\n                \"children_definition\": \"Consumers less than 16 years of age.\",\n                \"rights_of_children_details\": [\n                    {\n                        \"citations\": \"US CPRA Section 1798.120(c) , US CPRA Regulations § 7070 , US CPRA Regulations § 7071 , US CPRA Regulations § 7072\",\n                        \"details\": \"(1) A covered entity shall not sell the personal information of consumers if it has actual knowledge that the consumer is less than 16 years of age, unless \\n\\n(a) the consumer, in the case of consumers at least 13 and less than 16 years of age, or \\n\\n(b) the consumer's parent or guardian, In the case of consumers who are less than 13 years of age, \\n\\nhas affirmatively authorized the sale of the consumer’s personal information. \\n\\n(2) A covered entity that willfully disregards the consumer’s age shall be deemed to have had actual knowledge of the consumer’s age. \\n\\n(3) The covered entity shall establish a reasonable process for allowing such minor consumers or their parents or guardians to opt-in to the sale of their personal information. \\n\\n(a) For consumers below 13 years of age, methods that are reasonably calculated to ensure that the person providing consent is the child’s parent or guardian include, but are not limited to: \\n\\n(i) Providing a consent form to be signed by the parent or guardian under penalty of perjury and returned to the business by postal mail, facsimile, or electronic scan;\\n\\n(ii) Requiring a parent or guardian, in connection with a monetary transaction, to use a credit card, debit card, or other online payment system that provides notification of each discrete transaction to the primary account holder;\\n\\n(iii) Having a parent or guardian call a toll-free telephone number staffed by trained personnel;\\n\\n(iv) Having a parent or guardian connect to trained personnel via video-conference;\\n\\n(v) Having a parent or guardian communicate in person with trained personnel; and\\n\\n(vi) Verifying a parent or guardian’s identity by checking a form of government issued identification against databases of such information, as long as the parent or guardian’s identification is deleted by the business from its records promptly after such verification is complete.\\n\\n(b) For consumers at least 13 years of age and less than 16 years of age businesses shall have to establish, document, and comply with a reasonable process for allowing such consumers to opt-in to the sale or sharing of their personal information.\\n\\n(4) If the minor consumer or his parent or guardian opts in, the covered entity shall inform the consumer of the right to opt-out and of the process for doing so.\\n\\n(5) For consumers below 13 years of age, the consent obtained from their parents or guardians for the sale or sharing of personal information is in addition to any verifiable parental consent required under US Federal Law - COPPA. \\n\\n(5) A business shall also have to establish, document, and comply with a reasonable method, for determining that a person submitting a request to delete, request to correct, or request to know the personal information of a child under the age of 13 is the parent or guardian of that child. \\n\\n(6) A covered entity shall, in its privacy policy, include a description of the processes for minor consumers and their parents/guardians on how to opt-in for the sale and sharing of their personal information, to subsequently opt-out if they want and how parents and guardians of minors can submit requests to know, requests to delete and requests to correct personal information on behalf of their minors.\\n\\n(7) A business that exclusively targets offers of goods or services directly to consumers under 16 years of age and does not sell or share the personal information without the consent of consumers at least 13 years of age and less than 16 years of age, or the consent of their parent or guardian for consumers under 13 years of age, is not required to provide the Notice of Right to Opt-out of Sale/Sharing. of Personal Information\",\n                        \"links\": \"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.81.5.&part=4. , https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\"\n                    }\n                ]\n            },\n            \"state\": \"California\",\n            \"transparent_communication_requirement\": {\n                \"citations\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7003\",\n                \"details\": \"(1) Disclosures and communications to consumers shall be easy to read and understandable to consumers. For example, they shall use plain, straightforward language and avoid technical or legal jargon.\\n\\n(2) Disclosures required under this law shall also:\\n\\n(a) Use a format that makes the disclosure readable, including on smaller screens, if applicable.\\n\\n(b) Be available in the languages in which the business in its ordinary course provides contracts, disclaimers, sale announcements, and other information to consumers in California.\\n\\n(c) Be reasonably accessible to consumers with disabilities. \\n\\n(i) For notices provided online, the business shall follow generally recognized industry standards, such as the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, version 2.1 of June 5, 2018, from the World Wide Web Consortium. \\n\\n(ii) In other contexts, the business shall provide information on how a consumer with a disability may access the policy in an alternative format.\\n\\n(3) For websites, conspicuous links required under this law shall appear in a similar manner as other links used by the business on its homepages. For example, the business shall use a font size and color that is at least the approximate size or color as other links used by the business on its homepage.\\n\\n(4) For mobile applications, a conspicuous link shall be accessible within the application, such as through the application’s settings menu. It shall also be included in the business’s privacy policy, which must be accessible through the mobile application platform page or download page. \",\n                \"links\": \"https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\"\n            },\n            \"verification_process\": [\n                {\n                    \"citation\": \"US CPRA Regulations § 7060 , US CPRA Regulations § 7061 , US CPRA Regulations § 7062 , US CPRA Regulations § 7063\",\n                    \"details\": \"(1) A covered entity shall establish, document, and comply with a reasonable method for verifying that the person making a request to know or a request to delete or a request to correct is the consumer about whom the covered entity has collected information.\\n\\n(2) No burdensome verification may be required for consumers to exercise their opt-out rights. \\n\\n(3) In determining the method by which the covered entity will verify the consumer’s identity, the covered entity shall: \\n\\n(a) Whenever feasible, match the identifying information provided by the consumer to the personal information of the consumer already maintained by the covered entity, or use a third-party identity verification service that complies with this section. \\n\\n(b) Avoid collecting the types of personal information identified as sensitive or valuable personal information unless necessary for the purpose of verifying the consumer. \\n\\n(c) Consider the following factors:\\n \\n(i) The type, sensitivity, and value of the personal information collected and maintained about the consumer. Sensitive or valuable personal information shall warrant a more stringent verification process. \\n\\n(ii) The risk of harm to the consumer posed by any unauthorized access or deletion. A greater risk of harm to the consumer by unauthorized access or deletion shall warrant a more stringent verification process; \\n\\n(iii) The likelihood that fraudulent or malicious actors would seek the personal information. The higher the likelihood, the more stringent the verification process shall be; \\n\\n(iv) Whether the personal information to be provided by the consumer to verify their identity is sufficiently robust to protect against fraudulent requests or being spoofed or fabricated; \\n\\n(v) The manner in which the covered entity interacts with the consumer; and \\n\\n(vi) Available technology for verification. \\n\\n(4) A covered entity shall generally avoid requesting additional information from the consumer for purposes of verification. If, however, the covered entity cannot verify the identity of the consumer from the information already maintained by the covered entity, the covered entity may request additional information from the consumer, which shall only be used for the purposes of verifying the identity of the consumer seeking to exercise their rights, security, or fraud-prevention. The covered entity shall delete any new personal information collected for the purposes of verification as soon as practical after processing the consumer’s request.\\n\\n(5) A covered entity shall not require the consumer or the consumer’s authorized agent to pay a fee for the verification of their request to know, request to correct or request to delete. For example, a covered entity may not require a consumer to provide a notarized affidavit to verify their identity unless the covered entity compensates the consumer for the cost of notarization. \\n\\n(5) A covered entity shall implement reasonable security measures to detect fraudulent identity verification activity and prevent the unauthorized access to or deletion or correction of a consumer’s personal information. \\n\\n(6) If a covered entity maintains consumer information that is de-identified, a covered entity is not obligated to provide, delete or correct this information in response to a consumer request or to re-identify individual data to verify a consumer request. \\n\\n(7) If there is no reasonable method by which a business can verify the identity of the consumer to the degree of certainty required by this law:\\n\\n(a) the business shall state so in response to any request and explain why it has no reasonable method by which it can verify the identity of the requestor.\\n\\n(b) A business shall deny a request to know specific pieces of personal information if it cannot verify the identity of the requestor.\\n\\n(c) If the business has no reasonable method by which it can verify any consumer, the business shall explain why it has no reasonable verification method in its privacy policy. \\n\\n(d) The business shall evaluate and document whether a reasonable method can be established at least once every 12 months.\",\n                    \"link\": \"https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/cppa_regs.pdf\",\n                    \"methods\": \"(1) For account holders:\\n\\n(a) If a covered entity maintains a password-protected account with the consumer, it may verify the consumer’s identity through it's existing authentication practices.\\n\\n(b) The covered entity shall require a consumer to re-authenticate themself before disclosing or deleting the consumer’s data.\\n\\n(c)  If a covered entity suspects fraudulent or malicious activity on or from the password-protected account, it shall not comply with a consumer’s request to know or request to correct or request to delete until further verification procedures determine that the consumer request is authentic and the consumer making the request is the person about whom the covered entity has collected information.\\n\\n(2) For non account holders:\\n\\n(a) For Right of Access:\\n\\n(i) Covered entities are required to verify a consumer's identity to a reasonable degree of certainty for requests to know categories of personal information. A reasonable degree of certainty may include matching at least two data points provided by the consumer with data points maintained by the covered entity. \\n\\n(ii) Covered entities are required to verify a consumer's identity to a high degree of certainty for request to know specific pieces of personal information. A reasonably high degree of certainty may include matching at least three pieces of personal information provided by the consumer with personal information maintained by the covered entity that it has determined to be reliable for the purpose of verifying the consumer together with a signed declaration under penalty of perjury that the requestor is the consumer whose personal information is the subject of the request. If a covered entity uses this method for verification, the business shall maintain all signed declarations as part of its record-keeping obligations. \\n\\n(b) For Right to Erasure and Right to Correction: A covered entity must verify the identity of the consumer to a reasonable or reasonably high degree of certainty depending on the sensitivity of the personal information and the risk of harm to the consumer posed by unauthorized deletion or collection.\",\n                    \"time_extension\": \"\",\n                    \"time_period\": \"\",\n                    \"verification_applicable_dsrs\": [\n                        \"rightToAccess\",\n                        \"rightToRectification\",\n                        \"rightToErasure\"\n                    ]\n                }\n            ]\n        }\n    ]\n}"
            }
          ]
        },
